#
# Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica: Pseudo Time, Shadow Time, Retroactive Enchantment, Chaos Mathematics
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPseudoTime a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pseudo time: the present moment of Planck duration, with no connection between moments"@en, "Pseudozeit: der gegenwärtige Augenblick von Planck-Dauer, ohne Verbindung zwischen den Augenblicken"@de ;
    skos:definition "CMT has two forms of time: ordinary or pseudo time, created by memory and expectation, with no real existence apart from the present moment, and shadow time, which can be thought of as orthogonal to it. All forms of prescience and magic act through shadow time, since across pseudo time there is nothing but the present moment; every moment of pseudo time has its own past and future shadow time created from the etheric projections of its material phenomena, which are exactly what the Schrödinger wave equations describe. Pseudo time consists of moments of Planck duration, which perception lumps into subjective moments of about a tenth of a second at our most alert. Figures 3 and 4 show each moment as a point with a dumbbell of significantly probable shadow pasts and futures, time as pearls on a string, with no causal and no etheric connection between any two moments. All the paradoxes of quantum physics, of free will and determinism, and of enchantment and divination disappear when shadow time is placed orthogonally to pseudo time: Schrödinger's Cat can be both dead and alive only in shadow time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Pseudo Time, p. 29; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Pseudo Time, p. 30; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Pseudo Time, p. 32; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Pseudo Time, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShadowTime a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shadow time: an imaginary time dimension holding all possible pasts and futures as parallel universes"@en, "Schattenzeit: eine imaginäre Zeitdimension, die alle möglichen Vergangenheiten und Zukünfte als Paralleluniversen enthält"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work warns that visual analogy fails here, that language falters and even mathematics enters realms where its algebra no longer relates to experience, and it confines itself to a language-only exposition. It posits a universe that is a four-dimensional hypersphere, finite but unbounded in time and space, with an extra time dimension containing all possible etheric images of all pasts and futures, identified with the parallel universes of the wave equations. Wave functions have real effects and so consist of something, ether; taking a modulus multiplies a wave by its complex conjugate, the wave with the sign of the imaginary (time) coordinate reversed, and complex conjugate waves coming back to the present are what chaos magic theory demands. Imaginary time is a vast web of information whose contents are in theory available in the present; such a universe is constrained not by causality but only by self-consistency, effects creating their own causes through feedback loops, and the experienced now is an interference pattern of waves returning from multiple pasts and futures. The arrow of time follows from there being more dissipative futures than non-dissipative ones, and a longer-term mechanism favors information-rich systems such as living beings; the anthropic question is answered in the same way, a universe with life having more possible futures which reflect back their conjugate waves and encourage the conditions that make them possible."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Shadow Time, p. 33; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Shadow Time, p. 34; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Shadow Time, p. 36; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Shadow Time, p. 36; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Shadow Time, p. 38; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPrescienceKaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Prescience and clairvoyance by complex conjugate waves returning from shadow time"@en, "Vorauswissen und Hellsehen durch komplex konjugierte Wellen, die aus der Schattenzeit zurückkehren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Complex conjugate waves returning to the present on the real-time axis are exactly the mechanism by which prescience and clairvoyance occur, and they also carry back a shaping signal from shadow time to alter reality in both enchantment and retroactive enchantment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Shadow Time, p. 35; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Retroactive enchantment: an act of magic alters the past shadow time of a moment"@en, "Rückwirkende Verzauberung: ein magischer Akt verändert die vergangene Schattenzeit eines Augenblicks"@de ;
    skos:definition "Introduced as a new technique whose existence had been only vaguely suspected, retroactive enchantment is asserted by CMT to be possible; many bizarre and anomalous results recorded in the annals of magic can only have been due to it. In practice a spell is cast and some time later a result is recorded which strongly implies that an alteration has occurred to events that probably occurred before the spell was cast. Since past and future in pseudo time exist only as memory and expectation, the difficulty disappears: the act of magic alters the probability structure of the ether patterns in the past shadow time of a moment, so that a subsequent moment exhibits a present state and shadow future, which may also manifest physically later, other than might have been expected. As a psychological analogy, if you can convincingly alter your own memory you will modify your future actions."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: a result recorded some time after a spell which strongly implies that events prior to the spell were altered. The work gives no mark by which such a result is told from one that would have come without the spell, and names no condition of failure for the operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, introduction, p. 3; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpellcastingYieldsRetroactiveEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A spell can alter events that probably occurred before it was cast"@en, "Ein Zauber kann Ereignisse verändern, die wahrscheinlich vor ihm stattfanden"@de ;
    skos:definition "A spell is cast and some time later a result is recorded which strongly implies an alteration to events that probably occurred prior to the spell being cast; the act of magic alters the probability structure of the ether patterns in the past shadow time of a moment."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSpellcasting ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMemoryAlteration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Convincingly altering one's own memory"@en, "Das eigene Gedächtnis überzeugend verändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "One convincingly alters one's own memory; the work offers this as the psychological analogy of retroactive enchantment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicModifiedFutureActions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "One's future actions modified through an altered memory"@en, "Die eigenen künftigen Handlungen durch ein verändertes Gedächtnis verändert"@de ;
    skos:definition "A change in one's future actions brought about as a consequence of a convincingly altered memory."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMemoryAlterationYieldsModifiedFutureActions a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A convincingly altered memory modifies one's future actions"@en, "Ein überzeugend verändertes Gedächtnis verändert die eigenen künftigen Handlungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "If you can convincingly alter your own memory then you will modify your future actions as a consequence."@en ;
    skos:note "Offered as an analogy for retroactive enchantment, but stated as a claim and entered as one."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMemoryAlteration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicModifiedFutureActions ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPrescienceAsEnchantmentCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Prescience acts as enchantment: perceiving a possible event raises its probability"@en, "Vorauswissen wirkt als Verzauberung: ein mögliches Ereignis wahrzunehmen erhöht seine Wahrscheinlichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "CMT implies a symmetry between divination and enchantment: the very act of perceiving some event which might have occurred or might occur raises its probability. This is particularly a problem in prescience, not merely as self-fulfilling prophecy but because any image of the future tends to shape the future by etheric effect; the ancients often executed prophets of avoidable doom, and with good reason, for prescience can act as enchantment. The effect also works retroactively."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPrescienceKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 39; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelectingPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Modifying the future by selecting a perception of the past, and the past by the future"@en, "Die Zukunft durch Wahl einer Wahrnehmung der Vergangenheit verändern, und die Vergangenheit durch die Zukunft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because perceiving a possible event raises its probability, the future can also be modified by selecting a perception of the past, and vice versa."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Retroactive Enchantment, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosMathematics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos mathematics: sensitivity to initial conditions reaching down to the quantum level"@en, "Chaosmathematik: Empfindlichkeit gegenüber Anfangsbedingungen bis hinab auf die Quantenebene"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaos Mathematics, or Non-linear Dynamics, studies complex and unstable systems; its butterfly effect shows that a single butterfly changing direction must eventually alter the world weather pattern, and most such systems exhibit extreme sensitivity to initial conditions. It can give a false impression of hyper-determinism, but the sensitivity must reach down to the quantum level, placing such systems beyond causality; chaos mathematics describes the mechanisms by which quantum events, probabilistically influenced by etheric shadow time patterns, profoundly affect the macroscopic world."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Chaos Mathematics, p. 40; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Chaos Mathematics, p. 41; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica: The Equations of Magic
#

pmc:ChaosMagicEquationsOfMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The equations of magic: chance probability P and magic factor M, for planning enchantment and divination"@en, "Die Gleichungen der Magie: Zufallswahrscheinlichkeit P und Magiefaktor M, zur Planung von Verzauberung und Divination"@de ;
    skos:definition "The equations of magic are said to have been derived on a purely empirical basis to describe the probable effects of any act of magic. They apply mainly to enchantment and divination; their main use is in planning, for they indicate the precise requirements for any degree of probability manipulation, what must be put into an act and what is likely to come out. The effectiveness of an act depends on P, the probability of the desired effect occurring by chance, between zero (impossibility) and one (certainty), in divination the probability of guessing the correct answer by chance, and on M, the magic factor, made up of four components of any magical act: G, gnosis; L, magical link; A, conscious awareness; and R, subconscious resistance."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim of an empirical derivation names no data, trials or record. It is not entered as a receiving: the work claims no other speaker."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 41; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 42; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 42; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEquationsScopeCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The equations hardly describe evocation, invocation and illumination, and their factors are judged partly subjectively"@en, "Die Gleichungen beschreiben Evokation, Invokation und Erleuchtung kaum, und ihre Faktoren werden teils subjektiv geschätzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "More complex acts of magic such as evocation, invocation and illumination are not readily described by the equations except where their stated intent can be reduced to simple objectives corresponding to divination and enchantment. The quantification of certain factors awaits more precise calibration techniques, so they must currently be evaluated on a partly subjective basis, though the mathematics is called completely rigorous."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the work about the reach of its own instrument."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicEquationsOfMagic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 42; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 42; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFirstEquationOfMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The first equation of magic: M = G L (1-A)(1-R)"@en, "Die erste Gleichung der Magie: M = G L (1-A)(1-R)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Conscious awareness A and subconscious resistance R act negatively and are rarely eliminated entirely, so the magic factor is M = G L (1-A)(1-R), each factor evaluated from zero to one and M lying between zero and one. All four factors must be attended to in planning or the act will come to very little; M can never exceed the gnosis employed or the quality of the link, nor (1-A) or (1-R). If all four are at one half, M is a very poor 0.0625, unlikely to create any discernable effect whatsoever."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: with all four factors at one half the work predicts no discernable effect whatsoever. A numerical threshold, but on scales the work says are evaluated partly subjectively; the predicted outcome is observable, the input is not measured. The failure lies in the operator's factors."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFourFactorsBeforeMagicalAct a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "All four factors must be attended to in planning, or the act comes to very little"@en, "Alle vier Faktoren müssen in der Planung bedacht werden, sonst führt der Akt zu sehr wenig"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gnosis, magical link, conscious awareness and subconscious resistance must all be attended to in the planning of a magical act, or it will come to very little."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated of any magical act; the dependent step is entered as enchantment, and the same holds for divination. Not counted again as a condition of failure; the numerical case is counted at pmc:ChaosMagicFirstEquationOfMagic."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicFirstEquationOfMagic ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If all four factors are not attended to in the planning, the magical act will come to very little." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisKaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis: an extremely hysterical focusing of the mind, if only for a moment"@en, "Gnosis: eine äußerst hysterische Bündelung des Geistes, und sei es nur für einen Augenblick"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gnosis in the required range of 0.8 to 0.9 corresponds to an extremely hysterical focusing of the mind by ecstatic or meditative techniques, if only momentarily or intermittently; a good orgasm or the split second of quiescence at the end of half an hour's raja yoga may just suffice. There is no factor for strength of will in the equations; it is in meeting the conditions for gnosis that will is employed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOrgasm a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "A good orgasm as a momentary gnosis"@en, "Ein guter Orgasmus als augenblickliche Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "A good orgasm is given as an ecstatic technique that may just suffice for gnosis in the range 0.8 to 0.9."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrgasmYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A good orgasm may just suffice for gnosis"@en, "Ein guter Orgasmus kann für Gnosis gerade genügen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A good orgasm may just suffice for the extremely hysterical focusing of the mind that gnosis of 0.8 to 0.9 requires."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOrgasm ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRajaYoga a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Half an hour of raja yoga ending in a split second of quiescence"@en, "Eine halbe Stunde Raja-Yoga, die in einem Sekundenbruchteil der Stille endet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Half an hour's raja yoga, at whose end comes a split second of quiescence, is given as a meditative technique for gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRajaYogaYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The quiescence at the end of half an hour's raja yoga may just suffice for gnosis"@en, "Die Stille am Ende einer halben Stunde Raja-Yoga kann für Gnosis gerade genügen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The split second of quiescence at the end of half an hour's raja yoga may just suffice for gnosis in the range 0.8 to 0.9."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRajaYoga ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalLink a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical link: an elaborate memory image of the target changing, or an extensive image of it"@en, "Die magische Verbindung: ein ausgearbeitetes Erinnerungsbild des sich wandelnden Ziels oder ein umfassendes Bild davon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magical link in enchantment corresponds to an elaborate image in the memory of the target phenomenon undergoing the required change; the author has very little faith in hair and nail clippings alone. For a link in divination the best results come from an extensive mental image of the target, prior personal contact being infinitely preferable to a mere photograph. Only between phenomena or mental events of considerable similarity can a magical link operate, and the link has to be solved anew for each situation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Quantum Metaphysics, p. 24; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisBeforeEffectiveMagic a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis must be at 0.8 to 0.9 for magic to have any useful effect"@en, "Die Gnosis muss bei 0,8 bis 0,9 liegen, damit Magie überhaupt eine nützliche Wirkung hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "In practice gnosis needs to be in the range 0.8 to 0.9. If gnosis and link are kept there and conscious awareness and subconscious resistance at 0.1 to 0.2, an M of around 0.5 results, which is about the minimum required to have any useful effect in magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: an M of around 0.5 is called about the minimum required for any useful effect, so an act below it is predicted to have none. A numerical threshold on partly subjective scales with an observable outcome; the failure lies with the operator."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisKaos ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without gnosis in the range 0.8 to 0.9 the magic factor falls below about 0.5, the minimum required to have any useful effect in magic." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLinkBeforeEffectiveMagic a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical link must be at 0.8 to 0.9 for magic to have any useful effect"@en, "Die magische Verbindung muss bei 0,8 bis 0,9 liegen, damit Magie überhaupt eine nützliche Wirkung hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "In practice the magical link, like gnosis, needs to be in the range 0.8 to 0.9; only then, with awareness and resistance low, does M reach the minimum of about 0.5."@en ;
    skos:note "The same threshold as pmc:ChaosMagicGnosisBeforeEffectiveMagic, entered for the second prior step of the sentence and not counted again."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalLink ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without a magical link in the range 0.8 to 0.9 the magic factor falls below about 0.5, the minimum required to have any useful effect in magic." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 43; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEnsigilization a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ensigilization: a spell or sigil, meaningless to the conscious mind, depressing conscious awareness"@en, "Ensigilierung: ein Zauber oder Sigill, dem Bewusstsein bedeutungslos, das die bewusste Wahrnehmung senkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spell or ensigilization techniques are used to depress conscious awareness A to the range 0.1 to 0.2. A spell or sigil is an abstract representation of desire, constructed to be as meaningless to the conscious mind as possible; it channels the power of gnosis to the subconscious mind, which causes the desire to be realized by the emission or absorption of etheric patterns."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEnsigilizationYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A sigil channels gnosis to the subconscious, which realizes the desire"@en, "Ein Sigill leitet die Gnosis zum Unbewussten, das den Wunsch verwirklicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "A spell or sigil channels the power of gnosis to the subconscious mind, which causes the actual desire to be realized by the emission or absorption of etheric patterns."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEnsigilization ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSubconsciousResistanceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Subconscious resistance above 0.2 is a serious liability"@en, "Unbewusster Widerstand über 0,2 ist eine ernste Belastung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Subconscious resistance R must be depressed to the minimum, as anything above 0.2 is a serious liability. Much of the paraphernalia and theory of magic, including this theory, exists partly to convince the magician that he or she is a magician and that magic is possible in a cultural climate heavily antagonistic to such notions."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: resistance above 0.2 is named a serious liability. A numerical threshold on a partly subjective scale, naming no observable outcome; the failure is put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicFirstEquationOfMagic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSecondEquationOfMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The second equation of magic: Pm = P + (1-P) M^(1/P)"@en, "Die zweite Gleichung der Magie: Pm = P + (1-P) M^(1/P)"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second equation combines M and P into Pm, the probability of bringing about a desired effect by magic; conversely it is used in planning to calculate how much magic is required to raise a probability to an acceptable level. Figure 5 and Table 2 give Pm for values of P and M from zero to one. Moderate acts of magic, M = 0.5 to 0.7, have a proportionally greater effect on events of similar probability and only marginally improve events of P = 0.2 or below or 0.8 or above, so it is more productive to work on events in this range when beginning a career in magic. Any act of magic not totally hopeless improves any non-zero probability. Any M = 1 act raises any probability, even zero, to certainty, so in theory anything can be done by magic, though in practice the conditions for an M = 1 conjuration are often difficult to meet."@en ;
    skos:note "The exponent is damaged in the layer at both places the equation is printed; 1/P is read from the figures of Table 2, which it reproduces (P = 0.5, M = 0.4 gives 0.580). The figures of Table 2 are computed from the equation, not observed; the work offers them as predictions. The advice to beginners to work on events of moderate probability is a recommendation without a stated consequence and is kept here rather than as a presupposing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 44; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 45; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 46; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicParapsychologyTesting a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Parapsychology tested at low probabilities elicits an M rarely above 0.5"@en, "Parapsychologie, an niedrigen Wahrscheinlichkeiten geprüft, erreicht ein M selten über 0,5"@de ;
    skos:definition "Parapsychologists may note that statistically more impressive results are predicted if psychic powers are tested against events of higher probability than the Zener card guessing of only P = 0.2. The results of academic parapsychologists so far are said to suggest that at best they elicit an M factor from their subjects rarely exceeding 0.5, due to the unfavorable conditions of their experiments."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure in its excusing form: the weak results of academic parapsychology are put on the unfavorable conditions of the experiments, not on the claim. The only reference in the part to the results of trials; no study is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrdinaryStepsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Not taking ordinary steps toward the result challenges one's magic to fail"@en, "Wer keine gewöhnlichen Schritte zum Ergebnis tut, fordert seine Magie zum Scheitern heraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "If it is the result that the magician seeks, he or she should take all possible ordinary steps to increase the probability of the desired result occurring by chance alone, before and after using magic. To do otherwise is basically to subconsciously challenge your magic to fail, and it will usually oblige by doing so."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: neglect of ordinary means is named as a cause of failure, and the failure is put on the operator. The outcome is observable; no count or deadline."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTeleportation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Occasionally teleporting objects whose probability of spontaneous movement is virtually zero"@en, "Gelegentlich Gegenstände teleportieren, deren Wahrscheinlichkeit spontaner Bewegung praktisch null ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "It is said to be occasionally possible to teleport objects whose probability of spontaneous movement is virtually zero."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, pp. 47-48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMiracle a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The miracle: an M = 1 conjuration that makes even an impossible event certain"@en, "Das Wunder: eine Beschwörung mit M = 1, die selbst ein unmögliches Ereignis gewiss macht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any M = 1 act of magic raises any probability to certainty, including events of probability zero, for which Pm jumps from zero to one only at M = 1; in theory anything can be done by magic, and it would be possible to teleport an entire planet to another star system. Parapsychology is said to show that miracles prove nothing other than that some people can sometimes exercise miraculous abilities."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 47; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPerfectLinkBeforeMiracle a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Nothing less than a perfect link, L = 1, can yield a miracle"@en, "Nichts weniger als eine vollkommene Verbindung, L = 1, kann ein Wunder hervorbringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Nothing less than L = 1 can yield the M = 1 factor required for a miracle; it is in practice not feasible to form an effective magical link to an entire planet, let alone to its desired destination."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicMiracle ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalLink ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Nothing less than L = 1 can yield the M = 1 factor required for a miracle; without an effective link to a planet and its destination the planet cannot be teleported." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDesireScaleIndependence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Desire is scale independent: a trivial desire is as effective as an obsessive one"@en, "Der Wunsch ist maßstabsunabhängig: ein belangloser Wunsch wirkt so gut wie ein besessener"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is no factor for strength of will or desire in the equations; will is employed in meeting the conditions for gnosis. Desire is scale independent: as long as a suitable magical link exists, subconscious resistance is low and an appropriate spell mechanism removes conscious awareness, a trivial desire is as effective as an obsessive one, so long as it is activated by gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAccidentalMagicCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Accidental results: gnosis activating a desire other than the one selected"@en, "Ungewollte Ergebnisse: die Gnosis aktiviert einen anderen als den gewählten Wunsch"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because desire is scale independent there is always the strong possibility of accidental magical results: if the gnosis activates a desire other than the selected one, perhaps through poor spell procedures, an alternative result may manifest if a magical link exists."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the misfire, a result other than the intended one, is named with its cause, poor spell procedure, which puts it on the operator. The outcome is observable."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicEnsigilization ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCollectiveConjuration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Collective conjuration, whose scores are not cumulative"@en, "Gemeinsame Beschwörung, deren Werte sich nicht summieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The effects of a number of persons conjuring simultaneously or sequentially for a common objective never exceed the best result any one of them might achieve: if one magician achieves Pm = 0.85, any lesser value before, after or at the same time makes no difference, and scores are not cumulative. The only value of a collective conjuration is that it allows greater scope for someone to do something outstanding and that mutual assistance is often conducive to a better performance all round."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCollectiveConjurationYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Collective conjuration gives scope for one outstanding act and a better performance all round"@en, "Gemeinsame Beschwörung gibt Raum für einen herausragenden Akt und eine bessere Leistung aller"@de ;
    skos:definition "A collective conjuration allows greater scope for someone to do something outstanding, and mutual assistance is often conducive to the creation of a better performance all round."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCollectiveConjuration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 48; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRepeatedConjurationCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "There is little point in repeating a conjuration unless it can be done better or chance has improved"@en, "Eine Beschwörung zu wiederholen hat wenig Sinn, wenn sie nicht besser gelingen kann oder der Zufall sich verbessert hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is very little point in repeating a conjuration unless there is a chance of doing it better, or the probability of occurrence by chance alone has improved, or both."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution against repetition, not a condition of failure: the work says what repetition adds, not how a failed act is recognised."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThirdEquationOfMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The third equation of magic, for conjurations that prevent an event, and the netting of opposed magics"@en, "Die dritte Gleichung der Magie, für Beschwörungen gegen ein Ereignis, und die Verrechnung gegenläufiger Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The third equation describes the probable effects of conjurations launched to prevent an event occurring, almost invariably enchantment, there being very little point in decreasing the probability of a successful divination for yourself; the Pm it yields is lower than the chance probability P, and it inverts the lines of the graph (Figure 6). When conflicting acts of magic are performed to raise and to lower the probability of an event, the respective values of M are subtracted and the remainder of the larger entered in the appropriate equation: M = 0.6 for and 0.4 against is assessed by entering 0.2 in equation two, and M = 0.55 for and 0.89 against by entering 0.34 in equation three."@en ;
    skos:note "The formula of the third equation is garbled in the layer, in both places it is printed, and is not reconstructed here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPreventingAnEvent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Lowering the probability of an event by conjuration"@en, "Die Wahrscheinlichkeit eines Ereignisses durch Beschwörung senken"@de ;
    skos:definition "A conjuration launched to prevent an event occurring, which yields a probability lower than the probability of its occurrence by chance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpellcastingYieldsPreventingAnEvent a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "An enchantment launched against an event lowers its probability"@en, "Eine gegen ein Ereignis gewirkte Verzauberung senkt seine Wahrscheinlichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Conjurations launched to prevent an event occurring, almost invariably enchantment type work, yield a probability lower than the original probability of occurrence by chance."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSpellcasting ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPreventingAnEvent ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 49; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDifficultyOfMagicCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The equations are not good news: enchantment and divination are very difficult"@en, "Die Gleichungen sind keine gute Nachricht: Verzauberung und Divination sind sehr schwierig"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three equations are not particularly good news for the would-be magician: enchantment and divination are very difficult. Considerable effort and skill are needed even to bring an M of 0.5 to bear on a situation, and even this makes only a small difference when it comes to forcing the hand of chance; improving the conditions of one's existence by pure parapsychology requires the skilful application of quite extreme acts of magic."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the work against the expectations its own equations might raise."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicEquationsOfMagic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 50; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 51; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMasteredManeuvers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Once mastered, high gnosis and low awareness and resistance are always available"@en, "Einmal gemeistert, sind hohe Gnosis und geringe Wahrnehmung und geringer Widerstand stets verfügbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once the maneuvers necessary to achieve high gnosis and low conscious awareness and subconscious resistance have been mastered they are always available, and only the problem of the magical link has to be solved for each situation to permit effective magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 51; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicManeuversBeforeEffectiveMagic a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The maneuvers of gnosis, awareness and resistance are mastered first, the link solved for each case"@en, "Die Kunstgriffe von Gnosis, Wahrnehmung und Widerstand werden zuerst gemeistert, die Verbindung für jeden Fall gelöst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once the maneuvers for high gnosis and low conscious awareness and subconscious resistance have been mastered, only the magical link has to be solved for each situation to permit effective magic."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentKaos ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicMasteredManeuvers ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, The Equations of Magic, p. 51; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics
#

pmc:ChaosMagicThreeParadigms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The transcendental, materialist and magical paradigms"@en, "Das transzendentale, das materialistische und das magische Paradigma"@de ;
    skos:definition "All philosophies, creeds, dogmas and beliefs are variants of three great paradigms, the transcendental, the materialist and the magical, none ever wholly absent and rarely wholly distinct. Transcendentalism is basically religious, from primitive spiritism through pagan polytheism to monotheism and the theoretically non-theistic Buddhism and Taoism, its essence belief in spiritual beings or states superior to oneself, spirit being the ultimate reality. Materialism holds the universe to be entirely matter changing strictly by cause and effect, mind an electrochemical event and spirit a word without content. The pure magical paradigm suffers from insufficient vocabulary and is recovering from transcendental adulteration; its fundamental reality is ether, roughly mana, and all things happen by magic, the large-scale features of the universe having very strong etheric patterns that make them predictable but hard to influence by the patterns of thought. Magicians participate in nature, transcendentalists think themselves above it, materialists try to manipulate it. The universe tends to provide evidence for whatever paradigm one believes in, and non-dominant paradigms are always present as superstitions and fears."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 53; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 54; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 55; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThreeConceptsOfTime a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Time in the three paradigms: apocalyptic, linear and cyclic, and the magical doctrine of reincarnation"@en, "Die Zeit in den drei Paradigmen: apokalyptisch, linear und zyklisch, und die magische Lehre der Wiederverkörperung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Transcendentalists conceive time in millennial and apocalyptic terms, with a beginning and end initiated by spiritual beings, and hedge reproduction with taboo as a usurpation of divine power; apocalyptism is typically used to provoke revivals when business is slack. Materialist time is linear but unbounded, and the materialist may fear painful death but can have no fear of being dead. The magical view is that time is cyclic and all processes recur, all endings being beginnings, and this is reflected in the doctrine of reincarnation, which religious theories invariably contaminate with beliefs about a personal soul. From a strictly magical viewpoint we are an accretion rather than an unfolded unity: the psyche has no particular center, we are colonial beings, a collage of many selves, and as our bodies contain fragments of countless former beings, so does our psyche."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 56; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 57; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 57; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 58; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsycheTransfer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Adepts transferring large parts of their psyche in one piece"@en, "Adepten, die große Teile ihrer Psyche in einem Stück übertragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Certain magical traditions are said to retain techniques which allow adepts to transfer quite large amounts of their psyche in one piece, should they consider this more useful than dispersing themselves into humanity at large."@en ;
    skos:note "The techniques are not given in this part."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 58; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical self: no center, an assemblage of selves, free will from their disputes or from chance"@en, "Das magische Selbst: ohne Mitte, eine Ansammlung von Selbsten, freier Wille aus ihrem Streit oder aus dem Zufall"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transcendental self is spirit inserted into matter, stable if shared by consensus, but rival transcendentalisms threaten to disconfirm each other; the materialist self is the most problematic, caught between determinism and experienced free will and facing its own temporary duration, so materialist cultures show a frantic appetite for sensation and disposable beliefs. The chaos magical self rests on the same random chaos that makes the universe exist: it has no center and is an assemblage of parts, any number of which may temporarily club together and call themselves I; free will arises from a dispute between our selves or from the sudden random creation of a new option. Acts are conditioned, random, random choices between conditioned options, or conditional choices between random options, mostly in complex hierarchies of all four; as soon as we act one self proclaims that it did it. Each view disparages the others, and at last it is faith and taste: the transcendentalist has faith in a god self, the materialist in a reasoning self, and the selves of the magician have faith in each other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 58; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 59; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 60; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, p. 60; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychohistoryModel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The psychohistory model: dominant and opposition paradigms through the aeons"@en, "Das psychohistorische Modell: herrschende und oppositionelle Paradigmen durch die Äonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A simple succession of magical, transcendental and materialist paradigms lacks explanatory and predictive power; combining the linear time frame of materialism and transcendentalism with the cyclic one of magic gives a graph of dominant and opposition paradigms, the psychohistory model (Figure 7), which is qualitative and would need a non-linear time axis calibrated to each culture. Each paradigm tends to become an awesome tyranny at its zenith, while at its nadir its absence creates such difficulties that it persists as a ridiculed, barely tolerated or illegal opposition philosophy. Each expresses itself in a technology, hunter-gatherer in the shamanic aeon, agrarian in the religious, industrial in the rationalist, post-industrial in the coming one, and each aeon divides into two phases: animist and spiritist, pagan and monotheist, atheist and nihilist, and in the pandemonaeon the Chaoist sub-aeon and a phase left nameless."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 61; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritistPhase a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "From the empirical animist phase to the spiritist phase, where procedures part from their effects"@en, "Von der empirischen animistischen Phase zur spiritistischen, in der sich die Verfahren von ihren Wirkungen lösen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The animist phase of the shamanic aeon is dominated by magic and materialism: magic supplies the insight that all phenomena embody a mana which can be transferred or used to manipulate or anticipate them, a perfectly rational extrapolation and entirely empirical, certain procedures being performed and certain results usually following. Animism is prone to decay into the spiritist phase, in which magical theory becomes baroque as the decline of rational empiricism leads to a progressive divorce between magical procedures and their desired effects; theories and procedures proliferate as their effects and explanatory power become less certain, and ritual, myth, fetishism and taboo come to the fore."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work names a cause of magic ceasing to work at the level of a culture, the loss of empirical checking, and puts the failure on the theory and its practitioners rather than on the single operator. The proliferation of procedures as their effects grow less certain is the observable mark it gives."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 62; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 63; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReligiousAeon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The religious aeon: ritual negotiation with gods replaces magic, and monotheism replaces paganism"@en, "Das religiöse Äon: rituelle Verhandlung mit Göttern ersetzt die Magie, und der Monotheismus ersetzt das Heidentum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pagan cultures arise with settled agriculture and city-states: the powers recognized in shamanism are anthropomorphized into deities, personal power becomes a personal soul, and ritual negotiation with the gods replaces direct magical procedure. Paganism decays into monotheism, which fits the growing individual self, allows wider social control and an easier priesthood; the magic often expected of pagan priests is inherently difficult and unreliable except in the most talented hands and is not generally expected of monotheistic priests. Material and magical theories both oppose orthodoxy, so science and sorcery are often inseparable to their practitioners and persecutors; hybrids such as alchemy oppose monotheism, and magic disguises itself as theurgy, partly as camouflage and partly from metaphysical confusion."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the magic of pagan priests is called inherently difficult and unreliable except in the most talented hands, which puts the failure on the operator's talent; no observable mark is given."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRationalistAeon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The rationalist aeon: atheism decaying into nihilism, with magic reviving in opposition"@en, "Das rationalistische Äon: Atheismus, der in Nihilismus zerfällt, und Magie, die in der Opposition wiederauflebt"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the atheistic phase of the rationalist aeon transcendental theories give ground to material ones; purely magical theories virtually disappear, spiritualist occultism bears little relationship to magic and explains away any magic within it, and freemasonry, seeking mild transcendence through reason, is characteristic, as are humanism, communism and capitalism. Atheism decays into nihilism as materialism peaks and becomes sterile and tyrannical, and the dominant Western culture is said to be entering this phase. Magic and transcendentalism, both in opposition to materialism, are then often confused; a purely magical view, manifest in the growth of parapsychology, is on the increase, with neo-paganism, witchcraft and white light occultism as rebel philosophies and charismatic revival movements on the fringes of a decaying monotheism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 65; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 66; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 67; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPandemonaeon a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The pandemonaeon and its Chaoist sub-aeon: magic and materialism, then magic alone"@en, "Das Pandämonäon und sein chaoistisches Unteräon: Magie und Materialismus, dann Magie allein"@de ;
    skos:definition "The psychohistory model predicts that magic and the neo-religious themes mixed with it in its first revival will part company, the surviving magical traditions being those with no religious components, and that the nihilist phase will give way to a new aeon configured like the shamanic one, dominated first by materialist and magical beliefs and then by magic alone: the Pandemonaeon, its first phase the Chaoist sub-aeon. Advanced science already ascribes acausality, indeterminacy and observer dependence to reality, and biology, psychology and medicine must give ground to an emergent vitalism or morphic field equivalent to mana. The Chaoist age will be a truce between magic and science, magic first heavily camouflaged as science; transcendental theories will virtually disappear, magical phenomena will prove nothing spiritual, and the word God will be meaningless except to a few cliques and cranks. Its technology may be extremely complex and rationally incomprehensible, used as though magical, or a post-catastrophe technology for a new hunter-gatherer society; any credible stellar travel will have to rest on principles more akin to magic than science, some form of machine-enhanced teleportation perhaps sufficing. The second phase is left nameless."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 68; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 68; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 69; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAeonicStance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magician's stance toward the aeonic cycle, and politics as mere eddies on its tide"@en, "Die Haltung des Magiers zum äonischen Zyklus, und die Politik als bloße Wirbel auf seiner Flut"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician's stance toward the aeonic cycle depends on his or her attitude to change. Progress merely exchanges one set of problems for another, often larger; to campaign for or against change is to draw oneself into strife, though by nature we find turmoil more stimulating than abstaining. Having history on one's side brings radical company and perhaps the satisfaction of being proved right, but the defense of orthodoxy and vainglorious defeat have their own appeal. Politics, a squabble over the secondary codification of a society's primary values, affects only the timing of the cycle; democracy is due to industrialism, military technology and the weakening of monotheism, and the tide is caused by deep changes in our views of self and reality."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 70; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsychohistoricIntervention a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Working on the paradigms of a culture to hasten, impede or reverse its aeonic development"@en, "An den Paradigmen einer Kultur arbeiten, um ihre äonische Entwicklung zu beschleunigen, zu hemmen oder umzukehren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Armed with the psychohistoric model the magician can see which factors to work on in a culture or sub-culture. For monotheistic missionary work with pagans it is necessary to encourage both materialism and transcendentalism and to undermine magical theories; to combat such missionary work, or to revive a pagan cabal within a monotheist or atheist culture, one should do the opposite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSteeringAeonicDevelopment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hastening, impeding or reversing the aeonic development of a culture"@en, "Die äonische Entwicklung einer Kultur beschleunigen, hemmen oder umkehren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to hasten, impede or reverse aeonic development in a particular culture or sub-culture by working on the factors the psychohistoric model shows."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychohistoricInterventionYieldsSteering a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Working on the paradigms of a culture hastens, impedes or reverses its aeonic development"@en, "Die Arbeit an den Paradigmen einer Kultur beschleunigt, hemmt oder kehrt ihre äonische Entwicklung um"@de ;
    skos:definition "With the psychohistoric model the magician can readily see which factors to work on to hasten, impede or reverse aeonic development in a culture or sub-culture."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsychohistoricIntervention ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSteeringAeonicDevelopment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 71; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThreeTasksOfMagicalPhilosophy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Three tasks for the birth of the pandemonaeon: purge religion, camouflage rationally, undermine monotheism unseen"@en, "Drei Aufgaben für die Geburt des Pandämonäons: Religion tilgen, rational tarnen, den Monotheismus ungesehen untergraben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The primary concern of contemporary magic is hoped to be the safe and speedy birth of the pandemonaeon from within nihilist culture, and to assist it magical philosophy must strive for three things: first, to eliminate any remaining transcendental or religious concepts that still contaminate it, without throwing away any useful part of magic with them; second, to present its ideas and techniques with maximum rational camouflage, entering popular consciousness through a series of Trojan horses; third, as a precaution, to undermine the decaying remnants of monotheism without offering itself as a target."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, pp. 71-72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOfferingATargetCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Magic should undermine monotheism without offering itself as a target"@en, "Die Magie soll den Monotheismus untergraben, ohne sich selbst als Ziel anzubieten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magic should undermine the decaying remnants of monotheism without offering itself as a target. Parapsychology threatens fundamentalism by showing that miracles prove nothing but that some people can sometimes exercise miraculous abilities; idiotic satanic cults, on the other hand, provide very convenient enemy figures for fundamentalists, who often tend to invent them if they do not exist."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicThreeTasksOfMagicalPhilosophy ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDangerousTimesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Dangerous times ahead: apocalyptic belief and a nihilist rearguard may precede the pandemonaeon with catastrophe"@en, "Gefährliche Zeiten: apokalyptischer Glaube und eine nihilistische Nachhut könnten dem Pandämonäon eine Katastrophe vorausschicken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dangerous times lie ahead: millennial apocalyptic beliefs in monotheism may yet trigger disaster in the death spasms of transcendentalism, and materialist philosophies sliding into nihilism will mount a fierce rearguard action, demanding ever more of what is not working in an ecology unable to support it. The birth of the pandemonaeon could be long and bloody, and if things go badly it could be preceded by a catastrophe that precipitates a new stone age rather than an interstellar age; there will be niches for magicians in either."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPandemonaeon ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, The Psychohistoric Mechanism of the Aeons, p. 72; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEasternTraditions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Buddhism, Taoism and Heraclitus in the psychohistoric model, and the graded system of Tibet"@en, "Buddhismus, Taoismus und Heraklit im psychohistorischen Modell, und das gestufte System Tibets"@de ;
    skos:definition "Buddhism arose from rebellious speculation in the late pagan sub-aeon in the Orient, and similar developments in the first millennium B.C. led to Taoism and to pre-Socratic speculation, notably that of Heraclitus of Ephesus; the core principles of these non-theistic systems are said to have some relevance to an emerging magico-materialist synthesis. Taoism has tended to regress into superstitious ritual, Buddhism has appeared as de facto monotheism or as virtual paganism overlaid with transcendental theory, and Zen tries to recover the original teaching by severe austerity. In Tibet the fusion of Buddhism with shamanic traditions produced a graded system, Tantric or Vajrayana Buddhism, containing sorcery, shamanism, polytheism, transcendental monotheism, material causality and nihilism, held together by a benign ethics developed in a harsh environment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, Eastern Traditions in the Psychohistoric Model, p. 73; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 2 Aeonics, Eastern Traditions in the Psychohistoric Model, p. 74; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica
#

pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefAsTool a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Belief as a tool for achieving effects, the one meta-belief that makes a magician"@en, "Der Glaube als Werkzeug zur Erzielung von Wirkungen, der eine Meta-Glaube, der einen Magier macht"@de ;
    skos:definition "In Chaos Magic beliefs are not ends in themselves but tools for creating desired effects. It takes only the acceptance of a single belief to make someone a magician, the meta-belief that belief is a tool for achieving effects; this is often easier to observe in others than in oneself, people and whole cultures being enabled and disabled by their beliefs, which lead to activities that reconfirm them in a circle. Within the limits set by physical possibility, said to be wider and more malleable than most believe, one can make real any beliefs one chooses, including contradictory ones; the magician seeks no limited identity goal but the meta-identity of being able to be anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 75; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTerribleFreedomCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A terrible freedom: nothing is true, everything is permitted, and the consequences can be ghastly"@en, "Eine schreckliche Freiheit: nichts ist wahr, alles ist erlaubt, und die Folgen können grässlich sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "To fully realize that beliefs are tools is to face a terrible freedom in which nothing is true and everything is permitted, which is to say that everything is possible, there are no certainties, and the consequences can be ghastly. Laughter seems to be the only defense against the realization that one does not even have a real self."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefAsTool ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 75; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicChaosRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos ritual: acting as though a belief were true, fake it till you make it, then laugh it off"@en, "Chaosritual: so handeln, als wäre ein Glaube wahr, so tun, bis es gelingt, und ihn dann weglachen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The purpose of Chaos rituals is to create beliefs by acting as though such beliefs were true: in Chaos rituals you fake it till you make it, to obtain the power that a belief can provide. Afterward, if you have any sense, you laugh it off and seek the requisite beliefs for whatever you want to do next, as Chaos moves you. Gods are created by the act of ritually behaving as though they exist."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 75; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPowerOfBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The power that a belief can provide"@en, "Die Kraft, die ein Glaube verleihen kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "The power that a given belief can provide, obtained by acting as though the belief were true."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 75; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosRitualYieldsPowerOfBelief a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Acting as though a belief were true obtains the power it can provide"@en, "So zu handeln, als wäre ein Glaube wahr, erlangt die Kraft, die er verleihen kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "In Chaos rituals you fake it till you make it, to obtain the power that a belief can provide."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicChaosRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPowerOfBelief ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 75; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGodCreation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Creating or destroying any god, self or demon by investing or withdrawing belief"@en, "Jeden Gott, jedes Selbst oder jeden Dämon durch Zuwenden oder Entziehen von Glauben erschaffen oder zerstören"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaoism proclaims the death and rebirth of the gods: our subconscious creativity and parapsychological powers are more than adequate to create or destroy any god or self or demon or other spiritual entity that we choose to invest or disinvest belief in, at least for ourselves and sometimes for others as well. The results attained by creating gods by ritually behaving as though they exist are called frequently awesome. Humanity creates gods, not vice versa. The gods are dead; long live the gods."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, pp. 75-76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 1 Principia Magica, Quantum Metaphysics, p. 25; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosRitualYieldsGodCreation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ritually behaving as though a god exists creates it"@en, "Sich rituell so zu verhalten, als gäbe es einen Gott, erschafft ihn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gods are created by the act of ritually behaving as though they exist, with frequently awesome results."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicChaosRitual ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGodCreation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicUltimateRealityCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The results of created gods should not lead the magician to attribute ultimate reality to anything"@en, "Die Ergebnisse geschaffener Götter sollen den Magier nicht dazu bringen, irgendetwas letzte Wirklichkeit zuzuschreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The frequently awesome results of creating gods by ritual behavior should not lead the Chaos magician into the abyss of attributing ultimate reality to anything: that is the transcendentalist mistake, which narrows the spectrum of the self. The real awesomeness lies in the range of things we can discover ourselves capable of, even if we may temporarily have to believe the effects are due to something else in order to create them."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the text against what its own practice produces."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicGodCreation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGameOfReality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reality as an open-ended game, and the magician as one who sold his soul to play it more fully"@en, "Die Wirklichkeit als offenes Spiel, und der Magier als einer, der seine Seele verkaufte, um es voller zu spielen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magic appeals to those with much hubris and a fertile imagination and a strong suspicion that reality and the human condition have a game-like quality; the game is open ended and plays itself for amusement, players can make up their own rules to some extent and cheat by parapsychology, and the magic of the Psychonomicon consists of techniques that are extreme extensions of normal strategy within the game. A magician is one who has sold his soul for the chance of participating more fully in reality; only when nothing is true and the idea of a true self is abandoned does everything become permitted. There is some accuracy in the Faust myth, but Faust failed to take it to its logical conclusion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 76; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFirstStageCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The first stage of seeing through the game can end in weary cynicism or Buddhism"@en, "Die erste Stufe des Durchschauens des Spiels kann in müdem Zynismus oder im Buddhismus enden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first stage of seeing through the game of belief can be a shocking enlightenment that leads either to a weary cynicism or to Buddhism; only the second stage, applying the insight to oneself, destroys the illusion of the soul and creates a magician."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefAsTool ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicApplyingTheMetaBelief a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Applying to oneself the insight that belief is a tool"@en, "Die Einsicht, dass Glaube ein Werkzeug ist, auf sich selbst anwenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second stage of seeing through the game of belief is actually applying the insight to oneself; the realization that belief is a tool rather than an end in itself has immense consequences if fully accepted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBecomingAMagician a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The illusion of the soul destroyed and a magician created"@en, "Die Täuschung der Seele zerstört und ein Magier geschaffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The destruction of the illusion of the soul and the creation of a magician, by the acceptance of the single meta-belief that belief is a tool."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApplyingTheMetaBeliefYieldsBecomingAMagician a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Applying the insight to oneself can destroy the illusion of the soul and create a magician"@en, "Die Einsicht auf sich selbst anzuwenden kann die Täuschung der Seele zerstören und einen Magier schaffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second stage of actually applying the insight that belief is a tool to oneself can destroy the illusion of the soul and create a magician."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApplyingTheMetaBelief ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicBecomingAMagician ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMakingBeliefsReal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Making real any belief one chooses, contradictory beliefs included"@en, "Jeden gewählten Glauben wirklich machen, widersprüchliche eingeschlossen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Within the limits set by physical possibility, wider and more malleable than most people believe, one can make real any beliefs one chooses, including contradictory beliefs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicApplyingTheMetaBeliefYieldsMakingBeliefsReal a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The fully accepted meta-belief lets one make real any belief one chooses"@en, "Der voll angenommene Meta-Glaube lässt einen jeden gewählten Glauben wirklich machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The realization that belief is a tool, if fully accepted, has immense consequences: within the limits of physical possibility one can make real any beliefs one chooses, including contradictory ones."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicApplyingTheMetaBelief ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMakingBeliefsReal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMetaIdentity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The meta-identity of being able to be anything"@en, "Die Meta-Identität, alles sein zu können"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician is not one striving for any particular limited identity goal but one who wants the meta-identity of being able to be anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNothingIsTrue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Kali Yuga of the pandemonaeon: nothing is true, pure chance rules, and the greatest freedom is not being oneself"@en, "Das Kali Yuga des Pandämonäons: nichts ist wahr, reiner Zufall herrscht, und die größte Freiheit ist, nicht man selbst zu sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "The present is called the Kali Yuga of the pandemonaeon, wherein nothing is true and everything is permissible; it is better to build on shifting sands than on rock, philosophers are keepers of useful sarcasms, there is no secret of the universe, all is Chaos, evolution goes nowhere in particular, and pure chance rules the universe, which is why life is good. Solidity is an illusion, as is the foot that stands on it and most of all the self that thinks it owns either. Rather than let consumer preferences in the supermarkets of belief or sensation define a true self, one is urged to thieve boldly and lightheartedly from both, to sacrifice truth for freedom at every opportunity, since the greatest fun, freedom and achievement lies in not being yourself and Hell is the condition of having no alternatives; the chapter ends by urging the reader to face and surf the tidal wave of Chaos: create, destroy, enjoy, IO CHAOS."@en ;
    skos:note "The closing exhortation of Part 1. IO CHAOS is an acclamation and is not entered as a naming."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 77; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 78; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 78; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Liber Kaos, ch. 3 Principia Chaotica, p. 79; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Psychonomicon (part 2 of Liber Kaos), chapter 1, Practical Magic, and chapter 2,
# Sleight of Mind, in the order of the work.
#

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosAndEtherInferred a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos and the ether of shadow time are inferred, not perceived"@en, "Chaos und der Äther der Schattenzeit werden erschlossen, nicht wahrgenommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaos cannot be perceived or willed directly; its existence is inferred from the existence of matter and from the random behaviour of matter. The ether of shadow time is inferred in the same way from the reality of magic and from the stochastic behaviour of matter, which shows in its tendency to replicate form developed on a random basis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 1 Practical Magic, p. 83; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTautologyOfMatter a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The fourfold tautology of matter, the tetrahedron and the pentagram"@en, "Die vierfache Tautologie der Materie, das Tetraeder und das Pentagramm"@de ;
    skos:definition "Matter is divided for description into space, time, mass and energy, but each can only be described in terms of the other three, so every definition of matter is tautological; time is relative movement. Other sentient races might divide matter into a different number of parts. The consensus description on this world is the tetrahedron (figure 8), whose four vertices are what the ancients tried to formulate with air, water, earth and fire. With ether or spirit added it becomes the pentagram (figure 9), the simplest possible map of the universe, with Chaos itself omitted; the pentagram is also a symbol of magic, showing ether and matter interacting, and represents the five-dimensional description of Chaos Magic Theory, which adds etheric shadow time to the four common dimensions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 1 Practical Magic, pp. 83-84; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 1 Practical Magic, pp. 84-86; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMind a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sleight of mind: launching or receiving etheric patterns from below the threshold of awareness"@en, "Sleight of Mind: ätherische Muster von unterhalb der Bewusstseinsschwelle aussenden oder empfangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Psychonomicon confines itself to magic as the interaction of mind, which is called a material structure, with reality through the etheric medium, and says this can only be done by a series of techniques collectively called sleight of mind. The conscious mind is a maelstrom unable to hold one objective for a microsecond; the reader is invited to try holding attention on the dot at the end of the sentence without any other thinking. Sleight of mind means using the more stable thoughts, feelings, sensations and images stored in the subconscious or unconscious to launch or receive etheric patterns. Tricks are needed because such contents are not magically effective when brought into conscious focus, nor in their normal memory storage mode, an abstract code; they must be activated just below conscious awareness. Basic sleight of mind is to occupy the conscious mind with something that activates intent in the subconscious without consciously reminding the magician what it is. The technique is then treated for each of the five classical operations."@en ;
    skos:note "The dot exercise is offered as a demonstration that the conscious mind cannot hold a single object, not as a practice with an aim, and is kept inside this node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 1 Practical Magic, p. 86; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, pp. 87-88; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConsciousFocusAnnulsSubconscious a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Subconscious contents brought into conscious focus lose their magical effect"@en, "Unterbewusste Inhalte im Fokus des Bewusstseins verlieren ihre magische Wirkung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whatever in the subconscious is brought into the focus of the conscious mind will not be magically effective; neither is it effective in its normal memory storage mode. Hence the need for tricks."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work names conscious focus on the subconscious content as what makes it ineffective. It names no outcome the operator could observe and places the failure on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, pp. 87-88; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Sleight of mind in enchantment (pp. 88-90).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicStoneBatteringSpell a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Battering stones named for one's foes while hysterically angry, to cause dissension among them"@en, "Nach den Feinden benannte Steine in hysterischer Wut zusammenschlagen, um Zwietracht unter ihnen zu stiften"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work's example of a traditional spell: to cause dissension among one's foes, a number of stones are named with their names, or better some abstract form of their names, and battered together while the operator is hysterically angry. The anger partly blocks conscious thought and partly adds force to the subconscious desire. The anger is to be stimulated by some means other than thinking of the foes, and anything shouted during the battering should be consciously unintelligible; even the desire read backwards may suffice. The battered stones may afterwards be put in a pouch as a talisman to reinforce the spell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, pp. 88-89; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDissensionAmongFoes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dissension among one's foes"@en, "Zwietracht unter den eigenen Feinden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Causing dissension among one's foes is the stated purpose of the stone-battering spell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, p. 88; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStoneBatteringYieldsDissension a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The stone-battering spell is given as a means of causing dissension among foes"@en, "Der Steinschlagzauber gilt als Mittel, Zwietracht unter Feinden zu stiften"@de ;
    skos:definition "The battering of named stones in hysterical anger is presented as a spell to cause dissension among one's foes."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicStoneBatteringSpell ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDissensionAmongFoes ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, p. 88; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNoConsciousThoughtOfResult a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "During the magical act one must not consciously think or fantasize about the desired result"@en, "Während des magischen Akts darf man nicht bewusst an das gewünschte Ergebnis denken oder davon phantasieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "What many conventional texts fail to mention, the work says, is that during the magical act one must avoid consciously thinking or fantasizing about the desired result; the force driving the act must be raised by other means than thinking of its object."@en ;
    skos:note "The contrast with conventional texts names none of them; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicStoneBatteringSpell ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, pp. 88-89; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInhibitoryEnchantment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Inhibitory enchantment: stilling the mind by yoga-type exercises and sensory deprivation"@en, "Inhibitorische Verzauberung: den Geist durch yogaartige Übungen und Reizentzug stillen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Instead of an ecstatic means of preventing thought and channelling power to the subconscious, an inhibitory one may be used: the magician limits conscious attention entirely to the performance of the enchantment by yoga-type exercises and sensory deprivation that still the mind. This is said usually to be the more difficult approach for most magicians."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, p. 89; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTalismanRechargeCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Recharging a talisman without consciously thinking of what it represents"@en, "Einen Talisman wiederaufladen, ohne bewusst an das zu denken, wofür er steht"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the battered stones are kept as a talisman to reinforce the spell, the magician must avoid consciously thinking about whatever it represents whenever later attempting to charge it again."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicStoneBatteringSpell ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, p. 89; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicUnintelligibleRepresentationSpell a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Spells made by modifying a representation of desire until it is consciously unintelligible"@en, "Zauber, die eine Darstellung des Begehrens verändern, bis sie dem Bewusstsein unverständlich ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "All spells that work are said to be variants of the one basic technique and to work by the same mechanism; baroque systems of symbol and correspondence are generally unnecessary. Effective spells can be made simply by modifying written, drawn, modelled or spoken representations of desire until they become consciously unintelligible; the subconscious always knows what the resulting sigil, diagram, artifact or mantra is for."@en ;
    skos:note "The dismissal of systems of correspondence names no system; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, pp. 89-90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicForgottenSpellCollection a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Making up a collection of spells and performing them after their purpose is forgotten"@en, "Eine Sammlung von Zaubern anlegen und sie ausführen, wenn ihr Zweck vergessen ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magicians make up a collection of spells over a period and perform them at a later date, having consciously forgotten what they were for."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, p. 90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEffectiveEnchantment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Effective enchantment by sleight of mind"@en, "Wirksame Verzauberung durch Sleight of Mind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Bringing about a desired result by a spell whose representation the conscious mind does not recognise, as distinct from the particular aims of particular spells."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, pp. 89-90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicUnintelligibleSpellsYieldEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Consciously unintelligible spells and forgotten spells are said to give effective, often excellent, results"@en, "Dem Bewusstsein unverständliche und vergessene Zauber sollen wirksame, oft ausgezeichnete Ergebnisse bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Effective spells are said to be creatable by making a representation of desire consciously unintelligible, and excellent results are said often to be obtained by magicians who perform a collection of spells after consciously forgetting what they were for."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicUnintelligibleRepresentationSpell, pmp:ChaosMagicForgottenSpellCollection ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEffectiveEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Enchantment, pp. 89-90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Sleight of mind in divination (pp. 90-92).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMindInDivination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The three elements of divination, kept out of conscious awareness"@en, "Die drei Elemente der Divination, vom wachen Bewusstsein ferngehalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Divination has three elements: the target, the means of obtaining information about it, and the interpretation. The target must not enter conscious awareness while information about it is obtained, or the result will merely consist of ordinary thoughts, fantasies and guesses; the method too must preclude the interference of conscious thought. There are two basic methods for this, sortilege and hallucination. Whatever the method, the subconscious is to be thoroughly informed of the target and no conscious deliberation is to take place during the divination; the interpretation can then be made safely in full awareness afterwards, as a spell is planned beforehand."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: a target that enters conscious awareness turns the result into ordinary thoughts, fantasies and guesses. The outcome named is not one the operator can tell from a genuine result, and the failure is placed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, pp. 90-91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSortilege a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sortilege: shuffling cards, rolling dice, casting bones, sticks or coins"@en, "Sortilegium: Karten mischen, Würfel rollen, Knochen, Stäbe oder Münzen werfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sortilege procedures shuffle cards, roll dice, cast bones, sticks or coins and the like; the principle is that minute movements initiated by the subconscious provide a mechanism by which it can communicate its psychic knowledge."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, p. 90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicHallucinatoryDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Hallucinatory divination: gazing into a black mirror or a chalice of water"@en, "Halluzinatorische Divination: Blick in einen schwarzen Spiegel oder einen Kelch mit Wasser"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operator gazes into a black mirror or a chalice of water and waits for the subconscious to reveal its psychic knowledge by optical hallucination; other senses can also be used."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, p. 90; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTasteDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination by the predominant taste of a mixture of the four basic tastes"@en, "Divination durch den vorherrschenden Geschmack einer Mischung der vier Grundgeschmäcker"@de ;
    skos:definition "A mixture of the four basic tastes is drunk to see which predominates for a question, after an attribution has been fixed beforehand, for example sweet to yes, salt to probably, sour to probably not, bitter to no."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, pp. 90-91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBlackMirrorSigilDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Writing the target's name or sigil on the back of a black mirror and recording the visions"@en, "Den Namen oder das Sigill des Ziels auf die Rückseite eines schwarzen Spiegels schreiben und die Visionen aufzeichnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One effective hallucinatory technique: the name of the target, or better an abstract sigil representing it, is written on the back of a black mirror; any visions seen while gazing blankly into it are recorded by a machine or a scribe, and interpreted afterwards in full conscious awareness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, p. 91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicKnowledgeFromSubconscious a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Psychic knowledge obtained from the subconscious by divination"@en, "Durch Divination aus dem Unterbewussten gewonnenes psychisches Wissen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Information about a target that the subconscious is said to possess and to communicate through sortilege, hallucination or another sense when conscious thought is kept out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, pp. 90-91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDivinationMethodsYieldKnowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sortilege, hallucination, taste and the black mirror are said to convey the subconscious's psychic knowledge"@en, "Sortilegium, Halluzination, Geschmack und der schwarze Spiegel sollen das psychische Wissen des Unterbewussten vermitteln"@de ;
    skos:definition "Minute subconscious movements in sortilege, optical hallucination in mirror or chalice, the predominant taste of a mixture, and the black mirror with the target's sigil, called one effective technique, are each given as ways for the subconscious to communicate its psychic knowledge about a question."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSortilege, pmp:ChaosMagicHallucinatoryDivination, pmp:ChaosMagicTasteDivination, pmp:ChaosMagicBlackMirrorSigilDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicKnowledgeFromSubconscious ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, pp. 90-91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInformedSubconsciousBeforeDivination a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The subconscious thoroughly informed of the target before any divination"@en, "Das Unterbewusste vor jeder Divination gründlich über das Ziel unterrichtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whichever method is used, it is called important that the subconscious be thoroughly informed of the target and that no conscious deliberation take place during the divination."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as prescribed order: the sentence gives no consequence of an uninformed subconscious. The consequence the work does give, ordinary thoughts and guesses, belongs to the target entering awareness and is counted at pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMindInDivination."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicSortilege, pmp:ChaosMagicHallucinatoryDivination, pmp:ChaosMagicTasteDivination, pmp:ChaosMagicBlackMirrorSigilDivination ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMindInDivination ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, p. 91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpontaneousParapsychology a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spontaneous parapsychological events occur through sleight of mind; lust of result destroys magical effects"@en, "Spontane parapsychologische Ereignisse geschehen durch Sleight of Mind; Gier nach dem Ergebnis zerstört magische Wirkung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Careful observation is said to confirm that virtually all spontaneous parapsychological events occur through some form of sleight of mind: something just below the threshold of awareness initiates an unusual event or a half-sensed feeling that something is about to happen. The magician seeks to exploit this deliberately while avoiding doing it deliberately. Conscious lust of result destroys magical effects, so trickery must annul it and activate the subconscious."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: conscious lust of result is named as what destroys the effect. No outcome is named that the operator could observe; the failure is placed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, p. 91; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOmnipotenceMadnessCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Obsession and a feeling of omnipotence from sleight of mind, ending in madness"@en, "Besessenheit und Allmachtsgefühl aus Sleight of Mind, mit Wahnsinn am Ende"@de ;
    skos:definition "Developing sleight of mind for enchantment and divination brings dangers. One can become obsessed with what might lurk below the threshold waiting to be triggered by a stray analogical thought; a feeling of omnipotence can develop, particularly if divination is misread as enchantment and everything around one comes to seem the result of subconscious desire. The final madness begins when even the disasters that befall are read as what one must really have wanted. The only defence named against these pitfalls is to keep to the formal techniques of enchantment and divination, to ignore random results where possible or accept them with laughter, and to think positively at all times, since such thoughts permeate the subconscious."@en ;
    skos:note "The warning names its own misreading, divination taken for enchantment."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, pp. 91-92; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicParanoiaSpiralCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Paranoia as a downward spiral: subconscious fears realised by sleight of mind"@en, "Paranoia als Abwärtsspirale: unterbewusste Ängste durch Sleight of Mind verwirklicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Paranoia can become a vicious downward spiral: those who harbour subconscious fears of things going wrong or going against them will find it remarkably easy actually to make things go badly for themselves with even a small degree of expertise at sleight of mind. The same defence applies: formal technique, laughter at random results, positive thought."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicSleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Divination, p. 92; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Sleight of mind in evocation (pp. 92-94).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationBySleightOfMind a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation in three elements: implanting, empowering and directing the entity"@en, "Evokation in drei Elementen: das Wesen einpflanzen, ermächtigen und lenken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The entity is implanted in the subconscious either by an extended effort of fantasy and imagination or by a formal ritual in which it is visualised exercising the general powers the magician wants it to have. It is empowered, possibly as the climax of a ritual, by confining attention to its material basis, or to a sigil, mantra, glyph or other representation of it, while in full gnosis; sexual gnosis, symbolising the creation of a being, is called particularly appropriate. It is directed to tasks better by sleight of mind than by consciously meaningful commands: the command is made into a mantra or sigil and recited or visualised onto the material basis or image of the entity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Evocation, pp. 92-93; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFourServitors a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Four evoked entities: for complex enchantment, divination, defence and attack, and octarine work"@en, "Vier evozierte Wesen: für verwickelte Verzauberung, Divination, Abwehr und Angriff und oktarine Arbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four entities are said usually to suffice: one to execute complex enchantments, one for divinations where simple techniques may not suffice, one for magical defence and attack if necessary, and perhaps a fourth for works of Octarine Magic. Evoked entities are the magician's servitors."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Evocation, pp. 93-94; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEvocationYieldsServitors a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation is said to give servitors for enchantment, divination, defence and attack, and octarine work"@en, "Evokation soll Diener für Verzauberung, Divination, Abwehr und Angriff und oktarine Arbeit geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The implanted, empowered and directed entities are presented as the servitors that execute complex enchantments, divine where simple techniques fail, defend and attack, and work octarine magic."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFourServitors ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Evocation, pp. 93-94; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualGnosisDestructiveEntitiesCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Unwise to empower destructive entities by sexual gnosis"@en, "Unklug, zerstörerische Wesen durch sexuelle Gnosis zu ermächtigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For reasons deferred to the Sex Magic section, it is called generally unwise to empower entities with destructive capabilities by sexual gnosis."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Evocation, p. 93; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicServitorMasteryCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Evoked entities must not exceed the powers built into them, and their advice must not be taken"@en, "Evozierte Wesen dürfen die eingebauten Kräfte nicht überschreiten, und ihr Rat ist nicht anzunehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Evoked entities should never be allowed to exceed the powers the magician built into them, nor should extra capabilities be added to existing entities without careful consideration of the consequences. They are servitors and the magician their master; if the magician starts accepting advice from them, the results can be disastrous."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicEvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Evocation, p. 93; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Sleight of mind in invocation (pp. 94-97).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invocation in three stages: identification with a god-form, gnosis, possession"@en, "Invokation in drei Stufen: Identifikation mit einer Gottesform, Gnosis, Besessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocation is a three-stage process: the magician consciously identifies with a god-form, enters gnosis, and the subconscious then manifests the powers of the god-form. Two forms of subconscious activity must be brought into play together: the emotions, selectively aroused to add power, begun by deliberate simulation during identification, vital in gnosis and developing their own momentum in possession; and a faculty apparently located in the right cerebral hemisphere, induced to channel up the genius of what is invoked and give it form. The only certain technique is to prepare the ritual so that all physical materials, ideas and beliefs are available and then throw oneself into it wholeheartedly with a supreme effort of method acting: fake it till you make it. The gods, the archetypal forces, are said to be called up out of oneself and from the collective etheric of the human race."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: a successful invocation means nothing less than full possession by the god-form."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: getting more out than one appears to put in; the author is not satisfied unless surprised by the result, and only if the forces exceed expectations is the operation to be regarded as successful (p. 96). The two sentences state one criterion and are counted once."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, pp. 94-95; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, pp. 95-96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGodFormPossession a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Possession by a god-form, its powers manifest through the subconscious"@en, "Besessenheit durch eine Gottesform, deren Kräfte sich durch das Unterbewusste zeigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Full possession by the god-form, in which the magician's subconscious manifests the god-form's powers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 94; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationYieldsPossession a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Invocation is said to bring possession by the god-form"@en, "Invokation soll Besessenheit durch die Gottesform bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Identification, gnosis and method acting are said to end in the subconscious manifesting the powers of the god-form, full possession."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGodFormPossession ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 94; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAbbreviatedIdentification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Identification with a well-used god-form abbreviated to a moment's concentration"@en, "Die Identifikation mit einer vielgebrauchten Gottesform auf einen Augenblick der Konzentration verkürzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "With practice the first stage of conscious identification can be abbreviated greatly, until it may only be necessary to concentrate momentarily on a well-used god-form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 94; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRepeatedInvocationYieldsAbbreviation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Practice of invocation is said to shorten the identification stage"@en, "Übung der Invokation soll die Stufe der Identifikation verkürzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Repeated invocation of a god-form is said to abbreviate the stage of conscious identification to a momentary concentration."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAbbreviatedIdentification ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 94; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGodFormsAsArchetypes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "God-forms as archetypal manifestations of basic human drives, available by etheric resonance"@en, "Gottesformen als archetypische Erscheinungen menschlicher Grundtriebe, durch ätherische Resonanz verfügbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "God-forms may usefully be thought of as archetypal manifestations of basic human drives present in all individuals and available via etheric resonance from the acts and thoughts of all other humans. The pagans built the whole of human psychology into their pantheons, which is why classical pagan symbolism is so often used; but there is always enough sex, violence, love, brilliance and death going on in the world to resonate with for anyone who wishes to work free-form."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, pp. 94-95; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualShapesBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "We believe what we do more than we do what we believe: ritual as the shaper of belief"@en, "Wir glauben eher, was wir tun, als dass wir tun, was wir glauben: das Ritual als Former des Glaubens"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the most important tricks of invocation rests on the relation of ritual to belief: humans have a greater propensity to believe what they do than to do what they believe; all philosophy is biography, and those forced to perform military or religious ritual come to believe they are soldiers or devotees. The magician exploits this: starting from an idea of what one wants to believe, one selects a ritual and a god-form in which to act as though it were true, and so alters belief deliberately."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelectiveBeliefRange a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A range of beliefs invocable at will: lover, warrior, genius, businessperson, charismatic"@en, "Ein nach Belieben invozierbarer Vorrat an Überzeugungen: Liebhaber, Krieger, Genie, Geschäftsmensch, Charismatiker"@de ;
    skos:definition "A range of beliefs that can be invoked selectively as circumstances demand, so that one is capable of the actions that stem from believing oneself a superb lover, a courageous and efficient warrior, an intellectual genius, a brilliant businessperson, supremely likeable and charismatic, or anything else useful."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationYieldsBeliefRange a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual and god-form performed as if true are said to alter belief at will"@en, "Ritual und Gottesform, als wären sie wahr ausgeführt, sollen den Glauben nach Belieben verändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "By performing a chosen ritual and god-form as though a belief were true, the magician is said to alter belief deliberately and to acquire a range of beliefs to be invoked as circumstances demand."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelectiveBeliefRange ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 96; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExcessiveIdentificationCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Excessive identification with one successful god-form narrows humanity toward sterility and failure"@en, "Übermäßige Identifikation mit einer erfolgreichen Gottesform verengt das Menschsein bis zu Sterilität und Scheitern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The main danger of mastering invocation is excessive identification with a form that seems to yield good results. If an invoked form seems to dominate one's entire existence it is essential to try something else as well, preferably quite different; otherwise one faces a long-term narrowing of one's humanity, perhaps effective in the short term, that leads inexorably to sterility and failure."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the outcome of the neglected remedy is named, sterility and failure, after a short-term effectiveness. The outcome is named but not as a test the operator could apply on a date; the failure is placed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, pp. 96-97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEncroachingSelfCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A self enhanced by invocation that encroaches on the others is a sign that something is going wrong: a demon is a god acting out of turn"@en, "Ein durch Invokation gestärktes Selbst, das in die anderen übergreift, zeigt an, dass etwas schiefgeht: ein Dämon ist ein Gott, der aus der Reihe handelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician should be aware of god-forms that begin to exceed the purposes for which they were invoked. The author believes there are many selves within us, that we are all cases of multiple personality without the amnesia of the clinical condition; sanity is a state in which the component selves love and trust each other and let each other take control as circumstances demand. If a self enhanced by invocation begins seriously to encroach on the functions of the others, it is a sign that something is going wrong: the basic self-love binding the selves is breaking down and demons will arise. A demon is a god acting out of turn."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the encroaching self is named as the sign that something is going wrong, an outcome the operator could notice; the failure is placed on the operator's invocation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Invocation, p. 97; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Sleight of mind in illumination and in demonology (pp. 97-100).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicUsefulIllumination a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Illumination only as useful behavioural change, with measurable objectives"@en, "Erleuchtung nur als nützliche Verhaltensänderung, mit messbaren Zielen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only those forms of illumination that lead to useful behavioural changes deserve the name. The author reaches for a loaded wand at the word spirituality: some professionally spiritual people are vile and untrustworthy off duty because their beliefs conflict with basic drives and only distort their behaviour temporarily, and the demons come up out of the cellar at unexpected moments. Objectives are to be forms of self-improvement that can be precisely specified and measured and that change behaviour across one's whole existence. Invocation is the main tool; enchantment cast on oneself and divination to seek objectives may help; evocation can be used with care, but there is no point in creating an entity that is merely the repository of what we wish were true of ourselves, a frequent mistake in religion. Worship that creates only subconscious entities is inferior to wholehearted worship, which at best is pure invocation: the Jesuits' Imitation of Christ is more effective than merely praying to Jesus."@en ;
    skos:note "The remarks against spirituality and religion cite no text and are entered at the claim rather than as disputes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, pp. 97-98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicIlluminationBySleightOfMind a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Illumination: implanting identified beliefs by ritual acts, beginning with modest objectives"@en, "Erleuchtung: erkannte Überzeugungen durch rituelle Handlungen einpflanzen, mit bescheidenen Zielen beginnend"@de ;
    skos:definition "Illumination proceeds like invocation, except that the magician strives for specific changes to everyday behaviour rather than enhanced faculties drawn on for particular purposes. The required beliefs are identified and implanted in the subconscious by ritual or other acts, which force the subconscious acquisition of the beliefs they imply. Modest and realistic objectives are preferable to grandiose schemes: as we modify others by beginning with the most trivial demands, so with ourselves."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, pp. 98-99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBehaviouralChangeByImplantedBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Specific changes of everyday behaviour by implanted belief"@en, "Bestimmte Änderungen des Alltagsverhaltens durch eingepflanzten Glauben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Changes to everyday behaviour brought about by beliefs implanted through ritual acts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, pp. 98-99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFireWalkingAndPainObliviousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fire-walking and obliviousness to extreme pain by implanted belief"@en, "Feuerlaufen und Unempfindlichkeit gegen äußersten Schmerz durch eingepflanzten Glauben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Such feats as fire-walking and obliviousness to extreme pain are said sometimes to be achieved by the mechanism of implanted belief."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIlluminationYieldsBehaviouralChange a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Belief implanted by ritual is said to change behaviour and sometimes to permit fire-walking and obliviousness to pain"@en, "Durch Ritual eingepflanzter Glaube soll das Verhalten ändern und bisweilen Feuerlaufen und Schmerzunempfindlichkeit erlauben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ritual acts are said to force the subconscious acquisition of the beliefs they imply, and so specific changes of behaviour; fire-walking and obliviousness to extreme pain are said sometimes to be achieved by this mechanism."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicIlluminationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicBehaviouralChangeByImplantedBelief, pmc:ChaosMagicFireWalkingAndPainObliviousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, pp. 98-99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicUnsustainableBeliefCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Beware implanting beliefs the body or the environment cannot sustain"@en, "Hütet euch, Überzeugungen einzupflanzen, die Körper oder Umwelt nicht tragen können"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magicians should beware of implanting beliefs whose expression cannot be sustained by the human body or the environment: the belief that one can fly without an aircraft can be implanted, but rarely deeply enough to keep such flights from being of exceedingly short duration."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicIlluminationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOwnNemesisCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Belief implanted through ritual action: the most powerful weapon, which invokes one's own nemesis if used unwisely"@en, "Durch rituelle Handlung eingepflanzter Glaube: die mächtigste Waffe, die bei unkluger Verwendung die eigene Nemesis herbeiruft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sleight of mind that implants belief through ritual action is called more powerful than any other weapon of humanity and so pervasive that it is seldom noticed; it makes religions, wars, cults and cultures possible, has killed countless millions and created personal and social realities. Those who use it on others become messiahs or dictators according to their myopia; those who apply it to themselves have a jewel beyond price if they use it wisely, and otherwise tend rapidly to invoke their own nemesis with it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicIlluminationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Illumination, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLiberBoomerang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Boomerang: a god ignored is a demon born"@en, "Liber Boomerang: ein missachteter Gott ist ein geborener Dämon"@de ;
    skos:definition "Under Sleight of Mind in Demonology the short text Liber Boomerang warns against hypertrophying some selves at the expense of others: what is denied gains power and seeks strange and unexpected forms of manifestation. Deny death and other forms of suicide arise; deny sex and bizarre forms of its expression torment; deny love and absurd sentimentalities disable; deny aggression only to stare at the bloody knife in one's shaking hand; deny honest fear and desire only to create senseless neuroticism and avarice; deny laughter and the world laughs at you; deny magic only to become a confused robot, inexplicable even to oneself."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBySleightOfMind, pmp:ChaosMagicIlluminationBySleightOfMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 2 Sleight of Mind, Sleight of Mind in Demonology, Liber Boomerang, p. 100; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Psychonomicon, chapter 3, Auric Magic (pp. 101-106).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicAuricMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Auric magic: an exchange of information, not energy, between the subconscious and its environment"@en, "Aurische Magie: ein Austausch von Information, nicht Energie, zwischen dem Unterbewussten und seiner Umgebung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The occult systems disagree about the magical force said to reside in the body: Chi, Kia, Prana, Aura, the Subtle Body, Kundalini, Odic Force and the Chakras place it in different parts of the body, attribute divergent effects to it and offer endless methods. The author groups them as auric magic, after the Western term aura. Their effects cannot be explained by force or energy unless such forces may assume any properties; the author suggests auric magic does not work by these agencies but is a special case of enchantment and sometimes divination, working by an exchange of information between the subconscious, not the body, and its environment, which can include the body. Projected information selects the immediate future of a situation; received information can be felt as a bodily sensation although the body does not receive it. Movements, gestures, sounds, visualisations and simulated sensations are used as sigils are, to stimulate subconscious intent."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement of the systems is stated by the author and the systems are named but not cited; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 102; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicForceModelOfAuricMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Why auric magic is mistaken for a force, and the two drawbacks of the force model"@en, "Warum aurische Magie für eine Kraft gehalten wird, und die zwei Nachteile des Kraftmodells"@de ;
    skos:definition "Auric magic has been falsely held distinct from other magic because its development in martial arts and medicine made it seem a short-range effect of force and vitality, a view hardened by contemporary analogies of force fields, and because it often achieves results more impressive or repeatable than enchantment at a distance, which is due to the magical link being better at point-blank range. Practitioners who imagine a force will see effects that mimic an obliging force, but the model limits what is attempted and encourages ineffective methods: one can break an object without contact and even one whose location is unknown, and imagining an ectoplasmic force extruded from the body helps in neither case; the subconscious simply selects for reality a future in which the object is broken. Once force and energy are dropped, the effects are not limited by distance or intervening objects, providing a magical link can be established; close and distant magic alike depend on information instantaneously available everywhere."@en ;
    skos:note "The opposed view is that of unnamed practitioners; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 102-103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 105; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAuricActLinking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Linking a bodily act to an intent by repeated performance with visualisation of the result"@en, "Eine Körperhandlung durch wiederholte Ausführung mit Vorstellung des Ergebnisses an eine Absicht binden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The act, a posture, hand gesture, figure traced in the air, controlled breath with symbolic sound, or a deliberately simulated sensation, is performed repeatedly with the expectation or visualisation of the desired result until the two become inextricably linked in the subconscious. Great discipline is required to confine the imagination to successful outcomes although nothing is actually happening. Acts symbolic of the intent have some value, though too close a resemblance can bring conscious deliberation; in most martial arts the symbolic moves are also combat-effective in their own right."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 103-104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 104-105; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAuricMagicAct a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Performing the linked act with concentration on the act alone, to trigger the subconscious"@en, "Die gebundene Handlung in Konzentration allein auf die Handlung ausführen, um das Unterbewusste auszulösen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mechanism said to underlie every successful act of auric magic, and all the tales of wizards working wonders with a gesture: the conscious mind is occupied with performing an action strongly associated beforehand with an intent implanted in the subconscious. To do the magic, the act is performed with conscious concentration limited exclusively to its performance, which triggers the subconscious to project or receive the information that shapes reality to the goal."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 103-104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuricLinkingBeforeAct a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The act linked to the intent by repetition before it is performed as magic"@en, "Die Handlung durch Wiederholung an die Absicht gebunden, bevor sie als Magie ausgeführt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work says this is not as easy as it sounds: it is usually necessary to perform the act repeatedly with the visualisation of the result until both are linked, and only finally to perform it as the magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as prescribed order: the work says usually necessary and names no consequence of skipping the linking."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAuricMagicAct ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicAuricActLinking ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 103-104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBreakingObjectsWithoutContact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Breaking an object without contact, or whose location is unknown"@en, "Einen Gegenstand ohne Berührung zerbrechen, oder einen, dessen Ort unbekannt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "An object can be broken without physical contact, or with an unreasonably light blow, and even when its location is unknown; the less likely it is to break, the more difficult."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuricHealthAndVitality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Conferring health and vitality on oneself"@en, "Sich selbst Gesundheit und Lebenskraft verleihen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the powers named as often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuricHealingOthers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Healing others"@en, "Andere heilen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the powers named as often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCombatProwess a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Prowess in combat"@en, "Kampfkraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the powers named as often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPartialLevitationAndImmobility a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Partial levitation and its opposite, immobility"@en, "Teilweise Levitation und ihr Gegenteil, Unbeweglichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Partial levitation and its opposite of immobility, named among the powers often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBodyTemperatureControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Control of bodily temperature"@en, "Beherrschung der Körpertemperatur"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the powers named as often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEmpathicKnowingOfIntentions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Empathy to the point of knowing others' intentions and movements"@en, "Einfühlung bis zum Wissen um Absichten und Bewegungen anderer"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to empathise with others to the point of knowing their intentions and movements, named among the powers often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualAttractivenessAndCharisma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Projection of sexual attractiveness and charisma"@en, "Ausstrahlung sexueller Anziehung und Charisma"@de ;
    skos:definition "The projection of sexual attractiveness and charisma, named among the powers often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 104-105; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSubjectiveInvisibilityKaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Subjective invisibility, the ability to pass unseen"@en, "Subjektive Unsichtbarkeit, die Fähigkeit, ungesehen vorbeizugehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The opposite of projected attractiveness, subjective invisibility or the ability to pass unseen, named among the powers often sought through auric magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 105; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuricMagicYieldsPowers a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Auric acts are said to break objects at a distance and to confer the powers often sought: health, healing, combat, levitation, temperature, empathy, charisma, invisibility"@en, "Aurische Handlungen sollen Gegenstände auf Entfernung zerbrechen und die oft gesuchten Kräfte verleihen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The linked act performed with concentration is said to let the subconscious select a future in which an object breaks, and auric magic is said to be the means to the powers it lists as often sought: health and vitality for oneself, healing others, prowess in combat, partial levitation and immobility, control of bodily temperature, empathy to the point of knowing intentions and movements, projected sexual attractiveness and charisma, and subjective invisibility."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAuricMagicAct ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicBreakingObjectsWithoutContact, pmc:ChaosMagicAuricHealthAndVitality, pmc:ChaosMagicAuricHealingOthers, pmc:ChaosMagicCombatProwess, pmc:ChaosMagicPartialLevitationAndImmobility, pmc:ChaosMagicBodyTemperatureControl, pmc:ChaosMagicEmpathicKnowingOfIntentions, pmc:ChaosMagicSexualAttractivenessAndCharisma, pmc:ChaosMagicSubjectiveInvisibilityKaos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, pp. 104-105; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVagueSensationCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Simulating vague sensations of energy brings useless electric-shock sensations"@en, "Das Simulieren vager Energieempfindungen bringt nutzlose Empfindungen wie elektrische Schläge"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensation should only be simulated in the body for specific and well-defined purposes. An attempt to increase vitality by simulating feelings of energy in a vague general fashion will often lead to strange and useless sensations akin to electric shocks that merely disturb the body's normal functioning."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work names an outcome the operator would notice, useless shock-like sensations, and places it on the operator's vague simulation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAuricActLinking ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 105; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTooSymbolicActCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "An act too close to the intent invites conscious deliberation"@en, "Eine der Absicht zu ähnliche Handlung ruft bewusstes Abwägen hervor"@de ;
    skos:definition "Choosing acts symbolic of the intent has some value, but too close a resemblance can lead to problems with conscious deliberation; compromises often have to be made."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAuricActLinking ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 3 Auric Magic, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Psychonomicon, chapter 4, Eight Magics: introduction, Octarine Magic and Black
# Magic (pp. 107-116).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicEightMagics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The eightfold division of magic by the instinctual drives, denoted by colours"@en, "Die achtfache Teilung der Magie durch die Triebe, durch Farben bezeichnet"@de ;
    skos:definition "As perception and conception divide matter fourfold, the instinctual drives divide magic eightfold. The eight forms are denoted by colours of emotional significance (figure 10): octarine, pure magic; black, death magic; blue, wealth magic; red, war magic; yellow, ego magic; green, love magic; orange, thinking magic; purple or silver, sex magic. They could be attributed to the seven classical planets and Uranus for octarine, but the attribution is largely avoided in order to widen what can be attempted with each."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, p. 107; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, p. 109; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOctarine a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Octarine, the magician's personal perception of the colour of magic"@en, "Oktarin, die persönliche Wahrnehmung der Farbe der Magie durch den Magier"@de ;
    skos:definition "After Pratchett's hypothesis the eighth colour of the spectrum, the magician's personal perception of the colour of magic, may be called octarine. For the author it is an electric pinkish-purple in which his most significant optical visions occurred and with which he colours his more important spells and sigils on the astral; he takes it to follow from sex (purple) and anger (red) being his most effective forms of gnosis. Other magicians perceive it differently, and each is to seek out the colour of magic for themselves."@en ;
    skos:note "The author tells of a star ruby of exactly octarine hue given him by a wizard in India before he sailed an open boat through the Arabian Sea, and of shrieking conjurations to Thor and Poseidon in a typhoon among octarine lightning, after which he and his crew survived; he keeps the stone, uncertain whether it was a curse, a joke, a blessing or a test. Kept here as the author's own narrative; it claims no result for the conjuration and names no speaker of any wording."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 108; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 109; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicianSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magician-self: the octarine power flowering as a second centre beside the ego"@en, "Das Magier-Selbst: die oktarine Kraft, die als zweites Zentrum neben dem Ich aufblüht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The octarine power is the instinctual drive toward magic; allowed to flower it creates the magician-self in the psyche and an affinity with magician god-forms. Its general characteristics are antinomianism, partly from the culture's estrangement from magic, so that it takes an interest in everything that does not or should not exist by consensus, nothing being unnatural to it; and deviousness, an extension of the sleight of mind needed to manipulate the unseen, with a predilection for manipulation and the bizarre. In a civilian the octarine power is vestigial, and the resting mode is a mild yellow power taken for normal personality; the magician-self knows this is one of eight tools, so that the normal personality is its tool and vice versa, though it is not superior to the other selves. Its development gives the magician a second major centre to complement the ego; its awakening is sometimes called being bitten by the serpent, and those bitten recognise each other like lifeboat survivors. The greatest trick is to let magician-self and ego dance together without undue conflict. Its growth tends to make a mutant being in a paradigm few others know; it is hard to turn back, some try to abort the voyage with narcotics including mysticism, and it is a pilgrimage in which one wakes from one nightmare into another, the abysses being the initiations between worlds."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the magician who cannot disguise himself or herself as an ordinary person, or cannot act independently of his or her own ego, is called no magician at all. The criterion is an outcome others could observe, and it places the failure on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, pp. 109-110; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, pp. 111-113; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOctarineGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Octarine god-forms: Baphomet, Pan, Odin, Loki, Tiamat, Ptah, Eris, Hekate, Babalon, Lilith, Ishtar"@en, "Oktarine Gottesformen: Baphomet, Pan, Odin, Loki, Tiamat, Ptah, Eris, Hekate, Babalon, Lilith, Ischtar"@de ;
    skos:definition "The god-forms of the octarine power are those closest to the characteristics of the magician-self and usually the magician's most important modes of possession for purely magical inspiration; Baphomet, Pan, Odin, Loki, Tiamat, Ptah, Eris, Hekate, Babalon, Lilith and Ishtar are given as examples. A magician god-form may instead be formulated idiosyncratically, the serpent and the planet Uranus being useful starting points."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Baphomet", "Pan", "Odin", "Loki", "Tiamat", "Ptah", "Eris", "Hekate", "Babalon", "Lilith", "Ishtar" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form of the octarine power, magician god-form" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 110; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOctarineInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking the octarine power and magician god-forms"@en, "Die oktarine Kraft und Magier-Gottesformen invozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician invokes magician god-forms for the illumination of aspects of the magical self and for works of pure rather than applied magic; the octarine power is invoked to inspire the magician-self and to expand the primary personal arcana, the fundamental symbols, theories, kabbalas, obsessions and astral or physical weapons, by which one interprets and acts on reality magically, as distinct from the secondary arcana dedicated to the other powers. From the vantage of the octarine gnosis the magician-self should perceive the selves of the other seven powers and their interrelation within the organism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, pp. 110-111; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPureMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Works of pure magic: theories, training programmes, symbol systems, magical languages"@en, "Werke reiner Magie: Theorien, Ausbildungsprogramme, Symbolsysteme, magische Sprachen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Pure magic includes developing magical theories, philosophies and training programmes, devising symbolic systems for divination, spells and incantations, and creating magical languages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 110; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPrimaryArcanaExpanded a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An inspired magician-self and an expanded primary arcana"@en, "Ein inspiriertes Magier-Selbst und eine erweiterte primäre Arkana"@de ;
    skos:definition "The inspiration of the magician-self and the expansion of the fundamental symbols with which one interprets and interacts with reality magically."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 111; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychiatryOfSelves a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Some ability in psychiatry, the adjustment of the relations between the selves"@en, "Einige Fähigkeit in Psychiatrie, der Einstellung der Beziehungen zwischen den Selbsten"@de ;
    skos:definition "From the vantage of the octarine gnosis the magician-self perceives the other seven selves and their interrelation, and so the octarine power brings some ability in psychiatry, defined as the adjustment of the relationship between the selves of an organism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 111; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOctarineInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Octarine invocation is said to inspire the magician-self, expand the primary arcana, serve pure magic and bring some ability in psychiatry"@en, "Oktarine Invokation soll das Magier-Selbst inspirieren, die primäre Arkana erweitern, der reinen Magie dienen und einige Fähigkeit in Psychiatrie bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocation of the octarine power and its god-forms is given as the means to works of pure magic, to the inspiration of the magician-self and the expansion of the primary arcana, and, from the vantage of octarine gnosis, to some ability in the psychiatry of the selves."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOctarineInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPureMagic, pmc:ChaosMagicPrimaryArcanaExpanded, pmc:ChaosMagicPsychiatryOfSelves ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, pp. 110-111; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVPrime a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "V-Prime: writing magical languages in the native tongue without the verb to be"@en, "V-Prime: magische Sprachen in der Muttersprache ohne das Verb sein schreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaos magical languages are now usually written in V-Prime, Vernacular Prime, before transliteration into magical barbarous form: one's native tongue with every tense of the verb to be omitted, in accordance with quantum metaphysics. The nonsense of transcendentalism is said to disappear naturally once this is adopted: there is no being, all is doing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, pp. 110-111; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOctarineServitor a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Evoking an octarine servitor for magical research"@en, "Einen oktarinen Diener für magische Forschung evozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "An octarine servitor is evoked as a tool for magical research, its main functions usually being to assist in the discovery of useful information and contacts. Negative results are not to be ignored: the complete failure of a well-prepared servitor to retrieve information about the hypothetical cosmic big bang contributed to the development of the Fiat Nox theory."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the complete failure of a well-prepared servitor to retrieve information is named as an observable outcome. The work does not place it on the operator or the servitor but reads it as information about the world, evidence against the big bang."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 113; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicResearchInformationAndContacts a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Useful information and contacts for magical research"@en, "Nützliche Informationen und Verbindungen für magische Forschung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The discovery of useful information and contacts, named as the main function of an octarine servitor."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 113; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOctarineServitorYieldsInformation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "An octarine servitor is said to assist in discovering useful information and contacts"@en, "Ein oktariner Diener soll beim Auffinden nützlicher Informationen und Verbindungen helfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The evoked octarine servitor is called an invaluable tool for magical research, assisting in the discovery of information and contacts."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOctarineServitor ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicResearchInformationAndContacts ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Octarine Magic, p. 113; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathPrograms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Death programs as the price of sexual reproduction; the two conjurations of the black power"@en, "Todesprogramme als Preis der geschlechtlichen Fortpflanzung; die zwei Beschwörungen der schwarzen Kraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The death programs built into our genetic, behavioural and emotional structure are the price of sexual reproduction, which alone allows evolutionary change; only asexually replicating simple organisms are immortal. Two conjurations with the black power interest the magician: casting destruction spells and avoiding premature death."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, pp. 113-114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicChodRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Chod rite: a ritual rehearsal of death invoking the death-self"@en, "Der Chod-Ritus: eine rituelle Probe des Todes mit Invokation des Todes-Selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "So-called Chod rites are a ritual rehearsal of death in which, surrounded by all the symbols and paraphernalia of death, the magician invokes the death-self to manifest its knowledge and wisdom. The death-self, traditionally a black-robed skeletal figure with a scythe, is privy to the mysteries of ageing, senescence, morbidity, necrosis, entropy and decay, and often has a wry, world-weary humour. In the course of Chod rites the magician may experiment in shamanic style by invoking the visualised entities and symbols associated with diseases in order to practise banishing them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicResistingPrematureDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Resisting the manifestations of premature death by knowing the enemy"@en, "Den Erscheinungen des vorzeitigen Todes widerstehen, indem man den Feind kennt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The experience of the death-self and the black gnosis brings the knowledge of what it feels like to begin dying, and so prepares the magician to resist the manifestations of actual premature death."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathSelfDiagnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Uses of the death-self in medical diagnosis and divination"@en, "Verwendungen des Todes-Selbst in medizinischer Diagnose und Divination"@de ;
    skos:definition "By invoking and banishing the entities and symbols of diseases, the death-self is said to have some uses in medical diagnosis and divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChodRiteYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Chod rite is said to prepare resistance to premature death and to serve diagnosis and divination"@en, "Der Chod-Ritus soll auf den Widerstand gegen vorzeitigen Tod vorbereiten und der Diagnose und Divination dienen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The invocation of the death-self in the Chod rite is said to give the knowledge of beginning to die that prepares one to resist premature death, and, through the banishing of disease entities, some use in medical diagnosis and divination."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicChodRite ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicResistingPrematureDeath, pmc:ChaosMagicDeathSelfDiagnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEntropyRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Entropy rite: destruction spells cast from the vantage of the death-self"@en, "Der Entropie-Ritus: Zerstörungszauber vom Standort des Todes-Selbst aus gewirkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The death-self is invoked in the same general form as in the Chod rite, as a vantage point from which to cast destruction spells. The magician plants in the subconscious a mechanism by which the target could come to grief and projects it with a sigil or perhaps an evoked servitor; entropy magic works by sending the target information that encourages auto-destructive behaviour. It is performed with complete stealth in the cold fury of the black saturnine gnosis, a cold-blooded surgical strike without warning, aiming not at a fight but at a quick and efficient kill. Its supreme advantage is that targets rarely perceive the attack and blame only themselves and chance, so the assailant escapes reaction; its disadvantage is that invoices are hard to present for effects that seem natural."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, pp. 114-115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDestructionOfTarget a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bringing a target to grief by auto-destructive behaviour"@en, "Ein Ziel durch selbstzerstörerisches Verhalten zu Fall bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The destruction of a target, said to come about as the target's own auto-destructive behaviour encouraged by information sent to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEntropyRiteYieldsDestruction a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Entropy rite is said to bring a target to grief"@en, "Der Entropie-Ritus soll ein Ziel zu Fall bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A mechanism of grief planted in the subconscious and projected by sigil or servitor is said to encourage auto-destructive behaviour in the target."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEntropyRite ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDestructionOfTarget ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDestructiveMagicWeaknessCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Destructive magic as a position of weakness: always seek an alternative"@en, "Zerstörerische Magie als Position der Schwäche: stets eine Alternative suchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "One should always look for any possible alternative to destructive magic, for to be forced into having to use it is a position of weakness."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicEntropyRite ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, pp. 114-115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "God-forms of the black power: the cloaked skeleton, Charon, Thanatos, Saturn, Chronos, Hecate, Atropos, Anubis, Yama, Kali"@en, "Gottesformen der schwarzen Kraft: das verhüllte Skelett, Charon, Thanatos, Saturn, Chronos, Hekate, Atropos, Anubis, Yama, Kali"@de ;
    skos:definition "The god-forms of the black power are called legion: if the simple form of a cloaked skeleton with a scythe does not adequately symbolise the death-self, Charon, Thanatos, Saturn, Chronos, Hecate the Hag, Dark sister Atropos, Anubis, Yama and Kali may serve."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "cloaked skeleton with scythe", "Charon", "Thanatos", "Saturn", "Chronos", "Hecate the Hag", "Dark sister Atropos", "Anubis", "Yama", "Kali" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form of the black power, the death-self" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, pp. 115-116; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackServitorCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Servitors of the black power are dangerous to their owner and made for single tasks"@en, "Diener der schwarzen Kraft sind ihrem Besitzer gefährlich und werden für einzelne Aufgaben gemacht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Servitors of the black power are rarely established for long-term general use, partly because their use is likely to be infrequent and partly because they can be dangerous to their owner; they are made and dispatched for specific single tasks."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicEntropyRite ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Black Magic, p. 116; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Psychonomicon, chapter 4, Eight Magics: Blue Magic, Red Magic, Yellow Magic
# (pp. 116-128).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicWealthAsControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wealth as control of one's experiences, and money consciousness as the aim of wealth magic"@en, "Reichtum als Herrschaft über die eigenen Erfahrungen, und Geldbewusstsein als Ziel der Reichtumsmagie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Wealth is measured not in assets but in control over people and material and so ultimately over one's own experiences; money is an abstract concept quantifying economic activity, and wealth measures how well one controls one's experiences with money. Since varied and stimulating experiences tend to be expensive, the problem is to find an efficient money input that is itself agreeable. The aim of wealth magic is a large turnover of money that allows agreeable experiences at both the input and the output stage, which demands what is called money consciousness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 116; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMoneySpirit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Money as a spiritual being that likes to move"@en, "Geld als geistiges Wesen, das sich bewegen will"@de ;
    skos:definition "Money is said to have acquired all the characteristics of a spiritual being: invisible and intangible, coins, notes and electronic numbers being only its representations or talismans, yet able to create powerful effects. It has its own personality and tastes, avoids those who blaspheme it, flows toward those who treat it as it likes, and in a suitable environment reproduces itself. Its nature is movement; hoarded and unused it slowly dies, so it prefers to manifest as turnover. Money surplus to immediate pleasure should be reinvested as a further evocation; the truly money-conscious find that even their pleasures make money, and are by nature generous to an interesting investment but not to small cash handouts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, pp. 116-117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicWealthSelfInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking the wealth-self and the spirit of money"@en, "Das Reichtums-Selbst und den Geist des Geldes invozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attainment of money consciousness and the invocation of the wealth-self consist of acquiring a thorough knowledge of the predilections of the spirit of money and a thorough exploration of personal desires. Blue magic rituals may involve exorcisms of negative attitudes to wealth, divinatory explorations of one's deepest desires, and invocations of the wealth-self and the spirit of money in which the subconscious wealth level is adjusted by ritual expression of a new value and affirmations of new projects for investing resources and effort are made; hymns and incantations to money can be delivered, cheques for startling sums written to oneself, and desires proclaimed and visualised. Blue magic is the modification of one's belief about the wealth one should have through ritual enactment of alternative beliefs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, pp. 118-119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMoneyConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Money consciousness: real wealth manifesting effortlessly"@en, "Geldbewusstsein: wirklicher Reichtum, der sich mühelos einstellt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state in which, the predilections of money and one's own desires both understood, real wealth manifests effortlessly; money consciousness gets paid to enjoy itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWealthSelfInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge of the money spirit and of one's desires is said to make real wealth manifest effortlessly"@en, "Kenntnis des Geldgeistes und der eigenen Wünsche soll wirklichen Reichtum mühelos hervorbringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When both the predilections of the spirit of money and one's personal desires have been understood, real wealth is said to manifest effortlessly."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWealthSelfInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMoneyConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlueGnosisDemonsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The blue gnosis creates demons as easily as gods: anti-wealth"@en, "Die blaue Gnosis schafft Dämonen so leicht wie Götter: Anti-Reichtum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Wealth invocations must be handled with care: the blue gnosis of wealth and desire creates demons as easily as gods. Many contemporary success and sales training seminars create an hysterical desire for money and a hypertrophied desire for the mere symbols of wealth rather than the experiences people actually want; to work like a possessed maniac all day for the pleasure of drinking oneself into near oblivion on vintage champagne every night is to have missed the point entirely and to have entered a condition of anti-wealth."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: anti-wealth is named as the outcome of a mishandled wealth invocation, an outcome the operator could observe in his or her own days; the failure is placed on the operator."@en ;
    skos:note "The seminars are named as a class and not cited; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicWealthSelfInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, pp. 117-118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWealthBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "People have the degree of wealth they deeply believe they should have"@en, "Menschen haben den Grad an Reichtum, den sie zutiefst zu verdienen glauben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most of the poor in relatively free societies where others are rich owe their poverty to a lack of understanding of how money behaves or to negative feelings that repel it; neither intelligence nor investment capital is needed in any great degree to become wealthy, and the tales of the misery of the rich testify to a ridiculous myth among the poor. Most poor lottery winners, and only the poor enter regularly, have nothing to show for it a couple of years later, as if a subconscious force got rid of what they felt they did not deserve. People tend to have the degree of wealth they deeply believe they should have."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicExorcisingMoneyNegativity a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Examining and exorcising all negative thoughts and feelings about money"@en, "Alle negativen Gedanken und Gefühle über Geld prüfen und austreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "All negative thoughts and feelings about money are seriously examined and exorcised."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExorcismBeforeBlueMagic a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Negative feelings about money exorcised before any work of blue magic"@en, "Negative Gefühle über Geld vor jedem Werk blauer Magie ausgetrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before beginning works of blue magic it is called essential to examine all negative thoughts and feelings about money seriously and to exorcise them; the sentences that follow give the lottery winners who keep nothing as the case of a subconscious force ridding itself of wealth it feels undeserved."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as stated necessity because the work calls the step essential and gives the consequence in the next sentences; the consequence is read from the lottery example, which the work offers as illustration rather than as the result of a skipped exorcism."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicWealthSelfInvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicExorcisingMoneyNegativity ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Negative feelings about money tend to repel it, and wealth one feels one does not deserve is got rid of by a subconscious force, as with the lottery winners who have nothing to show a couple of years later." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWealthGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Wealth-self god-forms: Jupiter, Zeus, Midas, Croesus"@en, "Gottesformen des Reichtums-Selbst: Jupiter, Zeus, Midas, Krösus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Traditional god-forms with a prosperity aspect, such as Jupiter, Zeus and the mythical Midas and Croesus, can express the wealth-self."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Jupiter", "Zeus", "Midas", "Croesus" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form expressing the wealth-self" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMoneySchemeSpell a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Spells cast to enhance a scheme designed to make money"@en, "Zauber zur Förderung eines auf Gelderwerb angelegten Vorhabens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Simple money spells are rarely used in modern blue magic; the tendency is to cast spells designed to enhance schemes designed to make money. Serious blue magic is never attempted by conventional gambling, an expensive way of buying experiences unrelated to increasing wealth; blue magic is a matter of carefully calculated investment, and anyone but a fool should be able to devise an investment with better odds."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: if no mechanism is provided through which money can manifest, either nothing happens or the spell takes flesh by strange means, such as a legacy from the untimely death of a much-beloved relative. Nothing happening is an outcome the operator can observe; the failure is placed on the operator's omission of a mechanism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, pp. 119-120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEnhancedMoneyScheme a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A money-making scheme enhanced by spells"@en, "Ein durch Zauber gefördertes Vorhaben zum Gelderwerb"@de ;
    skos:definition "The success of a scheme designed to make money, furthered by spells cast for it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMoneySchemeSpellYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Spells cast on a money-making scheme are given as the modern means of blue magic"@en, "Auf ein Erwerbsvorhaben gewirkte Zauber gelten als das moderne Mittel blauer Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spells designed to enhance money-making schemes are presented as the means by which modern blue magic brings in money, provided a mechanism for its manifestation exists."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMoneySchemeSpell ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnhancedMoneyScheme ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMoneyMechanismCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A money spell without a mechanism may manifest through the death of a beloved relative"@en, "Ein Geldzauber ohne Mechanismus kann sich durch den Tod eines geliebten Verwandten erfüllen"@de ;
    skos:definition "If no mechanism is provided through which money can manifest, the spell may take flesh by strange means, such as a legacy from the untimely death of a much-beloved relative."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicMoneySchemeSpell ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Blue Magic, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWarAndMorale a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "War as self-selection of humanity, and morale as the decisive factor"@en, "Krieg als Selbstauslese der Menschheit, und Moral als entscheidender Faktor"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once humanity could defeat its predators it applied a fierce selection to itself by internecine war; opposable thumbs and tool handling, communication by sound, upright posture and the capacity to give and take commands were almost certainly selected for in millennia of armed conflict. Morality reflects this history: it is taboo to attack one's own tribe and a duty to attack foreigners, the only debate being who the tribe is; sport is war with extra rules. War is not without rules: it is fought to improve a bargaining position and to intimidate, not exterminate, and genocide is not war. Its structure reflects the fight-or-flight program; given rough parity of force, morale is the decisive factor in virtually any competitive, sporting or military encounter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 121; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicWarSelfInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking the war-self for vitality, aggression and morale"@en, "Das Kriegs-Selbst für Lebenskraft, Angriffslust und Moral invozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first aspect of red magic is the invocation of the vitality, aggression and morale that sustain one in any conflict, from life in general to outright war. Hybrid or idiosyncratic god-forms work as well as traditional ones; contemporary symbolism is not to be neglected, firearms and explosives being as welcome to the red gnosis as swords and spears; drums are virtually indispensable; sigils drawn in flammable liquids, or whole flaming circles in which to invoke, should be considered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 121; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVitalityAggressionMorale a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Vitality, aggression and morale to sustain oneself in any conflict"@en, "Lebenskraft, Angriffslust und Moral, um in jedem Konflikt zu bestehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The vitality, aggression and morale named as the aim of the war-self invocation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 121; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOverwhelmingGlamour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A glamour of vitality and potential aggression so overwhelming that combat magic is never needed"@en, "Ein Zauber der Lebenskraft und möglichen Angriffslust, so überwältigend, dass Kampfmagie nie nötig wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The real skill of red magic is said to be the presentation of such an overwhelming glamour of personal vitality, morale and potential for aggression that combat magic is never required."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWarSelfInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The war-self invocation is said to give vitality, aggression and morale, and at its best an overwhelming glamour"@en, "Die Invokation des Kriegs-Selbst soll Lebenskraft, Angriffslust und Moral geben, und im besten Fall einen überwältigenden Zauber"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocation of the war-self is given as the means of sustaining vitality, aggression and morale in conflict, and the real skill of red magic as a glamour that makes combat magic unnecessary."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWarSelfInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicVitalityAggressionMorale, pmc:ChaosMagicOverwhelmingGlamour ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 121; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWarGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "War-self god-forms: Ares, Ishtar, Ogoun, Thor, Mars, Mithras, Horus"@en, "Gottesformen des Kriegs-Selbst: Ares, Ischtar, Ogoun, Thor, Mars, Mithras, Horus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the god-forms in which the war-self can be expressed, Ares, Ishtar, Ogoun, Thor, Mars, Mithras and Horus are said to be often used."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Ares", "Ishtar", "Ogoun", "Thor", "Mars", "Mithras", "Horus" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form of the red power, the war-self" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 121; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCombatMagic a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Combat magic: open threat and curse, the adversary made paranoid and told the source"@en, "Kampfmagie: offene Drohung und Fluch, der Gegner paranoid gemacht und über die Quelle unterrichtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Combat magic is usually practised openly: the adversary is publicly threatened and cursed or receives an unpleasant-looking talisman, spell or rune. The aim is intimidation and control, so the adversary is to be made as paranoid as possible and informed of the origin of the attack. Otherwise it takes the general form of the Entropy rite, sigils and servitors carrying auto-destructive information to the target, but with sub-lethal intent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, pp. 121-122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIntimidationOfAdversary a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Intimidation and control of an adversary"@en, "Einschüchterung und Beherrschung eines Gegners"@de ;
    skos:definition "The intimidation and control of an adversary, made paranoid and carrying auto-destructive information with sub-lethal intent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCombatMagicYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Combat magic is said to intimidate and control the adversary"@en, "Kampfmagie soll den Gegner einschüchtern und beherrschen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Open curses, talismans, sigils and servitors are presented as the means of intimidating and controlling an adversary."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCombatMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicIntimidationOfAdversary ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Red Magic, pp. 121-122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicYellowPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The yellow power in four aspects: ego, charisma, laughter-creativity, assertion and dominance"@en, "Die gelbe Kraft in vier Gestalten: Ich, Charisma, Lachen-Kreativität, Durchsetzung und Dominanz"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most texts on so-called solar magic contradict each other or are internally confused, and astrological commentaries on the powers of the sun are called among the most idiotic nonsense the discipline can produce, because the yellow power has four related forms that schools of psychology have each emphasised alone: the ego or self-image, the mind's model of the general personality without most of the extreme behaviour the selves are capable of; charisma, the degree of self-confidence projected; laughter-creativity; and the urge to assertion and dominance. Success in most societies results from its skilful expression; its strength seems related to testosterone in both sexes, and in esoteric terms the Moon is the secret power behind the Sun. The ego accretes through the accidents of childhood and adolescence and then stays fairly constant even with dysfunctional elements; any invocation changes it somewhat, but direct work achieves much more. The yellow power is the root of most of the best and the worst of which we are capable."@en ;
    skos:note "The judgement on solar magic and astrology cites no text; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 122-123; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 123-124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 128; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOwningAPersonality a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Owning a personality rather than consisting of one"@en, "Eine Persönlichkeit besitzen, statt aus ihr zu bestehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The very recognition of the ego implies that change is possible; only those who realise that they own a personality, rather than consist of one, can modify it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 123-124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPersonalityInventory a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Preparing a detailed inventory of one's own personality"@en, "Ein ausführliches Verzeichnis der eigenen Persönlichkeit anlegen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A detailed inventory of one's own personality is prepared; for most people this is very difficult and unsettling, yet once it is done it is usually quite easy to decide what changes are desirable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOwnershipBeforeEgoChange a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Realising that one owns a personality before modifying it"@en, "Erkennen, dass man eine Persönlichkeit besitzt, bevor man sie ändert"@de ;
    skos:definition "Only those who realise they own a personality rather than consist of one can modify it; the inventory then makes the choice of changes easy."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantmentOfEgo, pmp:ChaosMagicEgoInvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicOwningAPersonality ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Those who do not realise that they own a personality, rather than consist of one, cannot modify it." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInventoryBeforeEgoChange a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The personality inventory before deciding on changes"@en, "Das Persönlichkeitsverzeichnis vor der Entscheidung über Änderungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once the inventory is done it is usually quite easy to decide what changes are desirable; the work of retroactive enchantment and invocation follows."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as presentation order: the work says the inventory makes the decision easy, not that the changes fail without it."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantmentOfEgo, pmp:ChaosMagicEgoInvocation ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicPersonalityInventory ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantmentOfEgo a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Retroactive enchantment of the ego: writing enabling memories beside disabling ones"@en, "Rückwirkende Verzauberung des Ich: ermächtigende Erinnerungen neben lähmende schreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Changes to the ego by magic are works of illumination accomplished mainly by retroactive enchantment and invocation. Retroactive enchantment here rewrites personal history: as history largely defines the future, the future changes when the past is redefined. Everyone can reinterpret what went wrong in a more favourable light but most fail to pursue it to the full. Disabling memories cannot be eliminated, but by visualisation and imagination parallel enabling memories of what might also have happened are written in to neutralise them, and where possible remaining physical evidence favouring the disabling memory is modified."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEgoInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invocations to modify the ego: personifying the new qualities in dress, speech, gesture and posture"@en, "Invokationen zur Änderung des Ich: die neuen Eigenschaften in Kleidung, Sprache, Gebärde und Haltung verkörpern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocations to modify the ego are ritual enchantments and personifications of the new desired qualities, with attention to planned changes of dress, tones of speech, gestures, mannerisms and body posture suited to the new ego."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMnemonicTriggerPersonality a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Practising an alternative personality on a mnemonic trigger such as moving a ring"@en, "Eine andere Persönlichkeit auf einen Erinnerungsauslöser hin üben, etwa das Umstecken eines Rings"@de ;
    skos:definition "A manoeuvre frequent in yellow magic: the manifestation of an alternative personality is practised with a specific mnemonic trigger, such as transferring a ring from one finger to another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 124-125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicModifiedEgo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A changed ego and with it a changed future"@en, "Ein verändertes Ich und mit ihm eine veränderte Zukunft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ego, self-image or personality changed toward desired qualities, and, by the redefinition of the past, a changed future."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 124; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEgoWorkYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Retroactive enchantment, ego invocation and the mnemonic trigger are said to change the ego and the future"@en, "Rückwirkende Verzauberung, Ich-Invokation und der Erinnerungsauslöser sollen Ich und Zukunft verändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Changes to the ego are said to be accomplished mainly by retroactive enchantment, which changes the future by redefining the past, and by invocation of the new qualities, with the practised alternative personality on a trigger as a frequent manoeuvre."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRetroactiveEnchantmentOfEgo, pmp:ChaosMagicEgoInvocation, pmp:ChaosMagicMnemonicTriggerPersonality ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicModifiedEgo ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 124-125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicYellowGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Yellow-power god-forms: Ra, Helios, Mithras, Apollo, Baldur"@en, "Gottesformen der gelben Kraft: Ra, Helios, Mithras, Apollon, Baldur"@de ;
    skos:definition "God-forms such as Ra, Helios, Mithras, Apollo and Baldur are useful to structure fresh manifestations of the ego and for experiments with the other three qualities of the yellow power."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Ra", "Helios", "Mithras", "Apollo", "Baldur" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form of the yellow power" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCharismaPretence a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Pretending to self-confidence, after a day or two of pretending to none"@en, "Selbstvertrauen vortäuschen, nach ein oder zwei Tagen vorgetäuschter Selbstunsicherheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Charisma, the projection of an aura of self-confidence, rests on a simple trick: after a short while there is no difference between pretended and actual self-confidence. One unsure how to begin pretending may spend a day or two pretending to absolutely zero self-confidence, which quickly reveals both the effectiveness of pretence and the specific thoughts, words, gestures and postures that project either pretence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfConfidenceCharisma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Charisma: a projected aura of self-confidence"@en, "Charisma: eine ausgestrahlte Aura des Selbstvertrauens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Self-confidence and charisma, the remedy for their lack."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCharismaPretenceYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Pretended self-confidence is said soon to become actual"@en, "Vorgetäuschtes Selbstvertrauen soll bald wirklich werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "After a short while there is said to be no difference at all between the pretence and the actuality of self-confidence, so pretending remedies a lack of confidence and charisma."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCharismaPretence ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelfConfidenceCharisma ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLaughterCreativity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Laughter-creativity: we laugh at our own forging of a new connection"@en, "Lachen-Kreativität: wir lachen über das eigene Schmieden einer neuen Verbindung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Humour depends on the sudden forging of a new connection between disparate concepts, and we laugh at our own creativity in forging it; the same elation comes from other creative activity, and a sudden insight brings laughter. Not laughing at a seriously brilliant piece of mathematics means not having understood it. It takes positive self-esteem to laugh at what is creatively funny; people of low self-esteem tend to laugh only at destructive humour and others' misfortunes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 125-126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicForcedLaughter a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Forcing hysterical laughter to end an enchantment or invocation"@en, "Erzwungenes hysterisches Lachen zum Abschluss einer Verzauberung oder Invokation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Laughter is often an important factor in invocations of the yellow god-forms; solemnity is not a prerequisite for ritual. Laughter also draws conscious attention away from sigils and other conjurations once finished with, and the deliberate forcing of hysterical laughter to end an enchantment or invocation, absurd as it may seem, is said to have been found remarkably effective in practice, a further sleight of mind that prevents conscious deliberation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConsciousAttentionWithdrawn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Conscious attention drawn away from a finished conjuration"@en, "Bewusste Aufmerksamkeit von einer abgeschlossenen Beschwörung abgezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Conscious deliberation about a sigil or conjuration prevented once the work is finished."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicForcedLaughterYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Forced laughter is said to be remarkably effective in drawing attention from a finished conjuration"@en, "Erzwungenes Lachen soll bemerkenswert wirksam die Aufmerksamkeit von einer abgeschlossenen Beschwörung abziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately forced hysterical laughter at the end of an enchantment or invocation is said to have been found remarkably effective in preventing conscious deliberation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicForcedLaughter ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicConsciousAttentionWithdrawn ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDominanceHierarchy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Human pecking orders, disguised but everywhere"@en, "Menschliche Hackordnungen, verborgen, aber überall"@de ;
    skos:definition "The pecking order in groups of social animals is obvious to us, yet dominance hierarchies are as prevalent in all human groups though we go to great lengths to disguise them; we belong to many groups with different status, often partly dependent on specialist ability. Given apparent competence in the ability a group requires, a person's position depends almost entirely on the assertion and dominance exhibited, mainly through nonverbal behaviour everyone understands subconsciously and few understand rationally, so that they cannot manipulate it deliberately. In military hierarchies the signals are exchanged deliberately only because of the capacity for direct coercion; the rules become internalised and permit mass self-sacrifice and slaughter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 126-127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 128; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDominanceBehaviour a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Deliberate dominance behaviour, delivered subtly and with increasing intensity"@en, "Bewusstes Dominanzverhalten, unauffällig und mit steigender Stärke eingesetzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Typical dominance behaviours are talking loudly and slowly, much eye contact, interrupting others while resisting interruption, an upright posture of concealed threat, invading others' personal space while resisting intrusion, placing oneself at the focus of attention, and initiating or pointedly refusing touch where touching is frequent; submissive behaviour is the reverse and appears spontaneously in response to successful dominance. Dominance signals are to be delivered subtly and with gradually increasing intensity, since most are exchanged subconsciously. Fake it till you make it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: dominance signals do not tend to work if their recipients perceive them consciously. The operator could notice the failure only in the other's response; it is placed on the operator's delivery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, pp. 127-128; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicControlByDominance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Control of others by dominance signals"@en, "Beherrschung anderer durch Dominanzsignale"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability some people have to control others, which the work says is nothing particularly occult, the signals being exchanged subconsciously."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHormoneChangeByBehaviour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Hormone levels modified by a deliberate change of behaviour"@en, "Hormonspiegel, durch bewusste Verhaltensänderung verändert"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is a two-way interaction between dominance behaviour and hormone levels; from a magical point of view, a deliberate change of behaviour will modify hormone levels."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDominanceBehaviourYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Deliberate dominance behaviour is said to modify hormone levels and to give control of others"@en, "Bewusstes Dominanzverhalten soll den Hormonspiegel verändern und Herrschaft über andere geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A deliberate change of behaviour is said to modify hormone levels, and successful dominance to call forth submissive behaviour in others spontaneously."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDominanceBehaviour ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicControlByDominance, pmc:ChaosMagicHormoneChangeByBehaviour ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Yellow Magic, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Psychonomicon, chapter 4, Eight Magics: Green Magic, Orange Magic, Purple Magic
# (pp. 128-141).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicGreenMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Green magic: the arts of making others friendly, loyal and affectionate"@en, "Grüne Magie: die Künste, andere freundlich, treu und zugetan zu machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because popular books overlap venusian love and lunar sex magic, planetary names are avoided, and green magic is confined to the arts of making other people friendly, loyal and affectionate toward oneself, though love magic is often performed for sexual objectives. Friends are probably our greatest asset; the author calls his address book his most valuable possession. Eliciting friendliness from people one likes is easy; making friendly those not disposed to friendship, or those one does not like, are valuable abilities. An unreciprocated friendship is a disability only to the person offering it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, p. 128; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGreenSelfLoveInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Green invocation beginning with self-love and proceeding to the loveability of all"@en, "Grüne Invokation, die mit Selbstliebe beginnt und zur Liebenswürdigkeit aller fortschreitet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocations to the green power begin with self-love, an attempt to see the wonderful side of every self one consists of, and proceed into a ritual affirmation of the beauty and loveability of all things and all people."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfLiking a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liking oneself, the ability that comes before others' liking"@en, "Sich selbst mögen, die Fähigkeit, die dem Gemochtwerden vorausgeht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to like oneself, which the work says can be enhanced by appropriate invocations of the green power."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, pp. 128-129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGreenSelfLoveInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Green invocation is said to enhance the ability to like oneself"@en, "Grüne Invokation soll die Fähigkeit, sich selbst zu mögen, stärken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ability to like ourselves, needed before others will like us, can be enhanced by appropriate invocations of the green power."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGreenSelfLoveInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelfLiking ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfLikingBeforeFriendship a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Liking ourselves before others will like us"@en, "Uns selbst mögen, bevor andere uns mögen"@de ;
    skos:definition "As with erotic attraction, it is first necessary to like ourselves before others will."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicFriendshipSpell, pmp:ChaosMagicBehaviouralMatching ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicGreenSelfLoveInvocation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Others will not like one who does not first like himself or herself." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, pp. 128-129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGreenGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Love-self god-forms: Venus, Aphrodite, Narcissus"@en, "Gottesformen des Liebes-Selbst: Venus, Aphrodite, Narziss"@de ;
    skos:definition "Suitable god-forms for the love-self include Venus, Aphrodite and the mythical Narcissus, whose myth is said merely to reflect a certain male prejudice against this type of invocation."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Venus", "Aphrodite", "Narcissus" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form of the green power, the love-self" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicFriendshipSpell a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Spells to make people friendly, cast from within the green gnosis"@en, "Zauber, um Menschen freundlich zu machen, aus der grünen Gnosis gewirkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "From within the green gnosis, spells to make people friendly may be cast by simple enchantment or by entities created for the purpose."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBehaviouralMatching a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Behavioural matching: mirroring the target's posture, movement, gaze and speech"@en, "Verhaltensangleichung: Haltung, Bewegung, Blick und Sprache des Gegenübers spiegeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "The empathic abilities stimulated by the green invocation work best face to face. Besides showing interest in everything the target says and affirming and sympathising with most of it, the critical factor is behavioural matching, which usually happens subconsciously: in the absence of overtly hostile postures, one matches the target's nonverbal behaviour precisely, the same posture, the same movements, the same degree of eye contact, speaking for similar intervals, but not moving to match at once; and one matches verbal behaviour, intelligence, social status and sense of humour."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: as with dominance behaviour, matching signals only work if the recipient does not consciously perceive them. The failure is placed on the operator's delivery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, pp. 129-130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFriendshipOfOthers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Making others friendly, loyal and affectionate, even those not disposed to it"@en, "Andere freundlich, treu und zugetan machen, auch die nicht dazu Geneigten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The friendship, loyalty and affection of others toward oneself, including those not disposed to friendship and those one does not like."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, pp. 128-129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFriendshipMagicYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Friendship spells and behavioural matching are said to make people friendly"@en, "Freundschaftszauber und Verhaltensangleichung sollen Menschen freundlich machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spells cast from the green gnosis by enchantment or entities, and above all the empathic face-to-face work of behavioural matching, are given as the means of making others friendly. The author says that practising these abilities while hitchhiking, before he made himself wealthy, soon had even people he found ghastly buying him lunch and carrying him far out of their way: empathy will get you anywhere."@en ;
    skos:note "The hitchhiking report is the author's own result and is kept here; it is not counted among the signs of success, which the work announces in advance to the practitioner."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicFriendshipSpell, pmp:ChaosMagicBehaviouralMatching ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFriendshipOfOthers ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Green Magic, pp. 129-130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrangePower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The orange power: charlatanry, trickery, living by one's wits, driven by fear"@en, "Die orange Kraft: Scharlatanerie, List, Leben vom Witz, von Angst getrieben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Charlatanry, trickery, living by one's wits and thinking fast on one's feet are the essence of the orange power, traditionally tied to the god-forms that were patrons of doctors, magicians, gamblers and thieves. Medicine has partly left charlatanry since antibiotics and hygienic surgery worked, yet about eighty percent of medications are still basically placebos and the caduceus remains its emblem; magic has likewise become less dependent on charlatanry with the quantum-probabilistic understanding of enchantment and divination and the abandonment of classical alchemy and astrology, pure magic being now an expression of the octarine power. Charlatanry still has its place: conjuring tricks were once the shamanic warm-up before placebo healing. Salesmen, confidence tricksters, stockbrokers and any profession with an extreme heart-attack rating draw on it. The motive power of the orange gnosis is a fear that does not inhibit but creates an extraordinary nervous speed producing quick moves and answers in tight corners."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, pp. 130-131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOrangeInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Orange invocation at frantic speed, deepened by mentally demanding tasks"@en, "Orange Invokation in rasender Geschwindigkeit, vertieft durch geistig fordernde Aufgaben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocations of the orange power are best delivered at frantic speed, and the gnosis is deepened by mentally demanding tasks such as adding long lists of numbers in one's head or ripping open envelopes containing difficult questions and answering them instantly, persisted with until a breakthrough to the experience of thinking without deliberation is achieved. Orange magic is usually restricted to invocations to enhance general quick-wittedness in secular activities such as gambling, crime and intellectual pursuits."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: the tasks are to be persisted with until a breakthrough to thinking without deliberation is achieved."@en ;
    skos:note "The author says the Fiat Nox hypothesis came together for him in the week following a particularly effective Mercury invocation by these techniques, and that in his experience enchantments and evocations performed after an orange invocation rarely give results as effective as the invocation itself. Both are his own report; the first names no speaker of any wording, and no pm:Receiving was entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMentalOverdrive a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mental overdrive: the fast response always forthcoming"@en, "Geistiger Overdrive: die schnelle Antwort ist immer zur Hand"@de ;
    skos:definition "The apotheosis of the wit-self is the ability to enter a state of mental overdrive in which the fast response is always forthcoming; it is created, paradoxically, not by thinking about thinking but by letting anxiety partially paralyse the inhibitory processes, so that the subconscious throws out a quick-witted response without conscious deliberation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, pp. 131-132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicQuickWittedness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "General quick-wittedness in gambling, crime and intellectual pursuits"@en, "Allgemeine Schlagfertigkeit in Glücksspiel, Verbrechen und geistiger Arbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Enhanced general quick-wittedness in secular activities such as gambling, crime and intellectual pursuits."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrangeInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Orange invocation is said to bring mental overdrive and general quick-wittedness"@en, "Orange Invokation soll geistigen Overdrive und allgemeine Schlagfertigkeit bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Frantic invocation deepened by demanding tasks is said to break through to thinking without deliberation and to enhance general quick-wittedness; the author reports a theory that came together in the week after such an invocation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOrangeInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMentalOverdrive, pmc:ChaosMagicQuickWittedness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrangeGodFormsNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Wit-self god-forms: Hermes, Loki, Coyote the Trickster, Mercurius"@en, "Gottesformen des Witz-Selbst: Hermes, Loki, Coyote der Trickster, Mercurius"@de ;
    skos:definition "Varied god-forms can give form to the wit-self; Hermes, Loki, Coyote the Trickster and the Roman Mercurius are often employed."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Hermes", "Loki", "Coyote the Trickster", "Mercurius" ;
    pm:nameRole "god-form of the orange power, the wit-self" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCrimeAndGamblingCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "For hotheads: thieves are caught by their addiction to anxiety, and gamblers who win are not gambling"@en, "Für Hitzköpfe: Diebe werden durch ihre Angstsucht gefasst, und Spieler, die gewinnen, spielen nicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Addressed to hotheads who may misunderstand what orange magic can do for crime and gambling. Theft is ludicrously easy if methodical, but most thieves are caught because they become addicted to the anxiety they feel as excitement and take risks to increase it, and once the anxiety of the theft fades only the anxiety of punishment remains, so they incriminate themselves; those cool enough to thieve successfully can make more from salesmanship. Of persistent gamblers the losers are those addicted to their own arrogance and those addicted to the anxiety of losing; the winners are not gambling at all, organising the odds, holding inside information or cheating, and this is true orange magic. Skilful poker includes not playing equals or armed men. Conventional gambling is set up so that only the most extreme psychic power makes a difference; the author would not bet on odds he had reduced from a hundred to one to sixty to one, though certain results with double blind-prescience on horse racing show encouraging potential."@en ;
    skos:note "Double blind-prescience is named and its results called encouraging, but the method is not described and no result is given; it is kept in this node and no practice was entered for it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicOrangeInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Orange Magic, pp. 132-134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexAndCults a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual teaching as control in cults and religions"@en, "Sexuelle Lehre als Herrschaftsmittel in Kulten und Religionen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Many cults in history have been led by a charismatic man able to persuade women to dispense sexual favours freely to other men, and many adepts past and present were whoremasters: the women are paid in the coinage of spirituality to service the men, who repay with adulation and accept the teaching, and the adulation increases the leader's charisma with the women in a positive feedback loop, a nice little earner until old age or a police raid, with high turnover as members find constant changes of partner against their interest in security and reproduction. Few religions lack a sexual teaching, which gives control; the durable ones trade on the suppression of free love, which secures women's position and men's paternity while adultery and prostitution flourish: brothels are built with the bricks of religion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 134-135; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualDissatisfaction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The built-in dissatisfaction of human sexuality, and turmoil as the source of achievement"@en, "Die eingebaute Unzufriedenheit der menschlichen Sexualität, und Aufruhr als Quelle der Leistung"@de ;
    skos:definition "People want to be told what to do with their sexuality because human sexuality has a built-in dissatisfaction function of evolutionary origin: genes in the female favour capturing the most powerful male available with clandestine liaisons, genes in the male favour impregnating as many females as he can support and a few others; concealed oestrus allows both adultery and pair bonding. The economic basis of a society presses for a type of sexuality, codified as morality that conflicts with biology, so nothing is perpetually satisfactory, neither celibacy, masturbation, monogamy, adultery, polygamy nor homosexuality. Much of art, culture, politics and technology arises from sexual yearning and dissatisfaction, and personal creativity and achievement are generally proportional to personal sexual turmoil: the art is the side-effect of a tempestuous sex life, the tensions of celibacy create fanatical religion. The Muse is the Muse only while one's relationship to her is unstable. Any sexuality eventually invokes the whole gamut of ecstasy, self-disgust, fear, delight, boredom, anger, love, jealousy, rage, self-pity, elation and confusion; to try to transcend them is to make oneself less than human. A dull sex life creates a dull person."@en ;
    skos:note "The rejection of transcendence of the passions names no teaching; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 135-136; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 136-138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSexualTurmoilInspiration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Inspiring oneself with maximum sexual turmoil and confusion"@en, "Sich mit größtmöglichem sexuellem Aufruhr und Verwirrung inspirieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Called the major but often unrecognised technique, and the major secret, of sex magic: one inspires oneself with maximum sexual turmoil and confusion in order to find out what one is capable of in other fields."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 137-138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCreativityAndAchievement a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Creativity and achievement in other fields"@en, "Kreativität und Leistung auf anderen Gebieten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Personal creativity and achievement, and greatness in any field, which the work ties to the propulsion of a turbulent emotional-sexual life."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 137-138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualTurmoilYieldsAchievement a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual turmoil is said to propel creativity and achievement"@en, "Sexueller Aufruhr soll Kreativität und Leistung antreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Maximum sexual turmoil is said to show what one is capable of in other fields, few achieving greatness without the propulsion of a turbulent emotional-sexual life."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSexualTurmoilInspiration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCreativityAndAchievement ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 137-138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOrgasmGnosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Orgasm as gnosis: implanting, empowering sigils, triggering invocation and divination"@en, "Orgasmus als Gnosis: einpflanzen, Sigillen ermächtigen, Invokation und Divination auslösen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first minor secret of sex magic. Anything held in the conscious mind at orgasm tends to reach down into the subconscious. At orgasm sigils for enchantment or evocation can be empowered by visualisation or by gazing at a sigil taped to the partner's forehead, though this is often more conveniently done auto-erotically. An invocation can be triggered at orgasm, particularly effectively if each partner assumes a god-form; the moments after orgasm are useful for divinatory vision seeking; prolonged sexual activity can lead to stages of trance useful in visual and oracular divination or in oracular possession."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 138-139; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualAbnormalitiesImplantedOrRemoved a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual abnormalities implanted or removed"@en, "Sexuelle Abweichungen eingepflanzt oder entfernt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sexual abnormalities are said to be readily implanted or removed by holding them in the conscious mind at orgasm."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Trance stages for oracular divination and possession"@en, "Trancestufen für orakelhafte Divination und Besessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Stages of trance reached by prolonged sexual activity, useful in visual and oracular divination or in oracular states of possession in invocation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 138-139; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrgasmGnosisYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Orgasm is said to implant or remove sexual abnormalities, empower sigils, trigger invocations and open trance"@en, "Der Orgasmus soll sexuelle Abweichungen einpflanzen oder entfernen, Sigillen ermächtigen, Invokationen auslösen und Trance öffnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "What is held in mind at orgasm reaches the subconscious, so sexual abnormalities can be implanted or removed and sigils empowered; an invocation triggered at orgasm is called particularly effective with god-forms on both partners; prolonged sexual activity leads to trance useful for oracular divination and possession."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOrgasmGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSexualAbnormalitiesImplantedOrRemoved, pmc:ChaosMagicEffectiveEnchantment, pmc:ChaosMagicGodFormPossession, pmc:ChaosMagicSexualTrance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 138-139; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrgasmEntropyCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Orgasmic gnosis is unwise for entropy or combat magic: leakage taints sexuality"@en, "Orgasmische Gnosis ist für Entropie- oder Kampfmagie unklug: Durchsickern verdirbt die Sexualität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Although the gnosis of orgasm can in theory serve any objective, it is generally unwise to use it for entropy or combat magic: no spell is ever totally insulated within the subconscious, and any leakage can implant quite detrimental associations with the sexuality."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the reason deferred at Sleight of Mind in Evocation (pmc:ChaosMagicSexualGnosisDestructiveEntitiesCaution)."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicOrgasmGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSexualGlamour a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual glamour as the offer of a reflection of the other, and the androgyny of attraction"@en, "Sexueller Zauber als Angebot eines Spiegelbilds des anderen, und die Androgynie der Anziehung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second minor secret of sex magic is the projection of sexual glamour, which depends on far more than appearance; some conventionally pretty people lack it entirely and some of the plainest have it to the limit. To be attractive one must offer something that reflects part of the other; if the offer becomes reciprocal it can lead to the sense of completion most readily celebrated by physical intimacy. Where the male shows a tough exterior and the female a softer persona, each in a sexual encounter seeks to reveal the concealed factor, compassion and vulnerability in him, inner strength in her; incomplete personalities, machismo to the core or its polar opposite, are never sexually attractive except transiently. Hence the philosophers of love find a certain androgyny important in attraction, the poetic ideal of a female soul in the male and a male soul in the female, which reflects the truism that to be attractive to others one must first become attracted to oneself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 138-139; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 139-140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMirrorAttractiveness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Practising being attractive in front of a mirror"@en, "Vor dem Spiegel üben, anziehend zu sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "A few hours spent practising being attractive in front of a mirror is called a valuable exercise."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMoonGlance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Moon Glance: a silver crescent visualised behind the eyes and a silver radiance beamed into the other's eyes"@en, "Der Mondblick: eine silberne Sichel hinter den Augen vorgestellt und ein silbernes Strahlen in die Augen des anderen gesandt"@de ;
    skos:definition "One briefly closes the eyes and momentarily visualises a silver lunar crescent behind them, the horns projecting out of each side of the head; then glances into the eyes of a potential lover while visualising a silver radiance beaming from one's eyes to theirs. The manoeuvre also dilates the pupils and usually causes an involuntary smile, both universal sexual signals, the first acting subconsciously."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAttractionOfALover a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Attracting a potential lover by projected sexual glamour"@en, "Einen möglichen Liebhaber durch ausgestrahlten sexuellen Zauber anziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attraction of a potential lover by projected sexual glamour."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMoonGlanceYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Moon Glance is said often to be effective in attracting a lover"@en, "Der Mondblick soll oft wirksam sein, einen Liebhaber anzuziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Moon Glance technique is called often effective, by way of dilated pupils and an involuntary smile."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMoonGlance ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAttractionOfALover ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfAttractionBeforeGlamour a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming attracted to oneself before projecting glamour"@en, "Sich selbst anziehend finden, bevor man Zauber ausstrahlt"@de ;
    skos:definition "To be attractive to others one must first become attracted to oneself: if one cannot get even mildly excited about oneself, one should not expect anyone else to get wildly excited."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicMoonGlance ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicMirrorAttractiveness ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If you cannot get even mildly excited about yourself, then do not expect anyone else to get wildly excited." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicConjuringSuitablePartners a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuring for suitable partners in general rather than a specific one"@en, "Für geeignete Partner im Allgemeinen beschwören statt für einen bestimmten"@de ;
    skos:definition "It is better to conjure for suitable partners in general, for oneself or others, than for a specific partner, since the subconscious usually has a far more subtle appreciation of who really is suitable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSuitablePartners a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Suitable sexual partners for oneself or others"@en, "Geeignete Sexualpartner für sich oder andere"@de ;
    skos:definition "Partners the subconscious judges suitable, attracted by a general conjuration."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConjuringSuitablePartnersYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A general conjuration is said to bring suitable partners"@en, "Eine allgemeine Beschwörung soll geeignete Partner bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Conjuring for suitable partners in general is recommended as the way to partners for oneself or others, the subconscious choosing better than conscious preference."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicConjuringSuitablePartners ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSuitablePartners ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpecificPartnerCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Unwise to cast spells for the attraction of a specific partner"@en, "Unklug, Zauber zur Anziehung eines bestimmten Partners zu wirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "It is generally unwise to cast spells for the attraction of specific partners."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicConjuringSuitablePartners ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicColoursOfSex a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Purple, silver, and black as the secret colour of sex"@en, "Purpur, Silber, und Schwarz als geheime Farbe des Sexus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sex magic is traditionally associated with purple for passion and silver for the Moon, but the effectiveness of black clothing as a sexual or anti-sexual signal, by style and cut, shows black to be in a sense the secret colour of sex, reflecting the biological and psychological relation of sex and death."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Purple Magic, pp. 140-141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Psychonomicon, chapter 4: Rites of a Mixed Nature, the Thanateros Ritual and An
# Azathoth Conjuration (pp. 141-151).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicRitesOfMixedNature a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Which powers combine in invocation and which are best kept apart"@en, "Welche Kräfte sich in der Invokation verbinden und welche besser getrennt bleiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The yellow power combines well in invocation with any other except the black, drawing the allied force more fully into the self-image. Green and purple invocations and enchantments often work well together; otherwise the forces are best used in isolation, though purple and black rites can have unusual effects and need not be excessively hazardous if carefully constructed, as in the Thanateros Ritual and the Azathoth Conjuration, the latter a work of octarine magic to be used infrequently and with caution."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Rites of a Mixed Nature, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBlackYellowDeathRebirth a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Black-yellow invocation in two halves: ritual sacrifice and re-creation of the self-image"@en, "Schwarz-gelbe Invokation in zwei Hälften: rituelles Opfer und Neuschaffung des Selbstbildes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Black-yellow invocations are conventionally performed in two halves as death-rebirth experiences, in which the magician seeks to re-create the self-image substantially after its ritual sacrifice."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Rites of a Mixed Nature, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfImageRecreated a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The self-image substantially re-created after its ritual sacrifice"@en, "Das Selbstbild nach seinem rituellen Opfer weitgehend neu geschaffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A self-image re-created after a ritual death, the aim of the black-yellow invocation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Rites of a Mixed Nature, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackYellowYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The black-yellow death-rebirth is said to re-create the self-image"@en, "Der schwarz-gelbe Tod und die Wiedergeburt sollen das Selbstbild neu schaffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The two-part death-rebirth invocation is the conventional means of re-creating the self-image after its ritual sacrifice."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBlackYellowDeathRebirth ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelfImageRecreated ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Rites of a Mixed Nature, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNeitherNeitherMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The neither-neither meditation: an idea, its opposite, both together, neither"@en, "Die Weder-noch-Meditation: eine Idee, ihr Gegenteil, beide zugleich, keines von beiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Called an oriental discipline for reaching between concepts to tap the subconscious power locked in them and release it in a burst of inspiration and illumination: one meditates for a time on an idea, existence and non-existence being often chosen, then on its polar opposite, then on the simultaneous presence of both, and finally on the simultaneous absence of both, which forces conception beyond its normal limits."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: illumination arrives if the fourth stage, the simultaneous absence of both qualities, can be completed. The work states no outcome for failing the fourth stage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, The Thanateros Ritual, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStateBeyondParadox a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The exalted, ecstatic, magical and creative state beyond paradox"@en, "Der erhabene, ekstatische, magische und schöpferische Zustand jenseits des Paradoxes"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state beyond paradox reached by the neither-neither effect, a burst of inspiration and illumination whose culmination in the Thanateros Ritual is ecstatic laughter, experienced for itself or used to cast a spell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, The Thanateros Ritual, pp. 141-142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicThanaterosRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Thanateros Ritual: a lunar-saturnine rite of illumination rehearsing sex, death and both at once"@en, "Das Thanateros-Ritual: ein lunar-saturnischer Erleuchtungsritus, der Sexus, Tod und beides zugleich probt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A celebration of Chaoist principles and a momentary invocation of the power of Chaos, exploiting the paradoxes of the death of self in sex and the birth of self in the encounter with death. It consists of a statement of intent, It is our will to experience Chaos through ritual rehearsal of sex and death, followed by three litanies each followed by an invocation, of Eros, of Thanatos, and of Chaos with both. It may be spoken by one or more participants or played as a recording; the others visualise the figures during each litany, and during each invocation begin hyperventilation and visualise the approach of the figures, ending with loud cries: an orgasmic moan for Eros, a scream of death-terror for Thanatos, and for Chaos a bizarre combination of both followed by ecstatic laughter. It is performed standing with eyes closed, gesticulating at will, and participants are encouraged to reach gnostic emotional release and to make heartfelt noise, above all at each culmination. The litanies say that in orgasm the self is lost and Chaos reminds us with a joke that we are nothing, that facing death the self is overwhelming and we are everything, and that in Chaos both are jokes and laughter is the reward of existence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, The Thanateros Ritual, pp. 141-143; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, The Thanateros Ritual, pp. 143-147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThanaterosYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The neither-neither meditation and the Thanateros Ritual are said to trick consciousness into the state beyond paradox"@en, "Die Weder-noch-Meditation und das Thanateros-Ritual sollen das Bewusstsein in den Zustand jenseits des Paradoxes überlisten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ritual is said to allow the magician to trick consciousness into the exalted, ecstatic, magical and creative state beyond paradox by the neither-neither effect, and the meditation to release the power locked in concepts as inspiration and illumination."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicThanaterosRitual, pmp:ChaosMagicNeitherNeitherMeditation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicStateBeyondParadox ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, The Thanateros Ritual, pp. 141-142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThanaterosFiguresNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Eros, Thanatos and Chaos, the three figures of the Thanateros Ritual"@en, "Eros, Thanatos und Chaos, die drei Gestalten des Thanateros-Rituals"@de ;
    skos:definition "The figures visualised and invoked in the Thanateros Ritual: Eros, a lover, real or imagined, who created us in the chaotic conjunction of genetic roulette; Thanatos, a robed skeleton with scythe, whose bargain is accepted; Chaos, both of these together."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Eros", "Thanatos", "Chaos" ;
    pm:nameRole "figures invoked in the Thanateros Ritual: lover, death, and both at once" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, The Thanateros Ritual, pp. 143-147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAzathothNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Azathoth, the egregore of sentience reaching for the stars, an elder god"@en, "Azathoth, die Egregore des nach den Sternen greifenden Bewusstseins, ein älterer Gott"@de ;
    skos:definition "Azathoth is called an egregore associated with the emergence of sentience from the primeval slime and its quest to reach for the stars, in other star systems too, the next nearest apparently Deneb in Cygnus. It has no shape or name meaningful to humans but responds to Azathoth, Atazoth and occasionally Astaroth, the last, a confused derivative of the Babylonian Ishtar, to be avoided. Certain alchemists knew it, and their name Azathoth means an increase of azoth, increasing etheric or morphic fields. H. P. Lovecraft encountered it and fearfully described it as the Blind Mad God at the Center of Chaos: blind because it prefers to manifest in an operator whose eyes are closed, mad because it communicates telepathically or from the subconscious only while the operator performs glossolalia. It is one of the elder gods, powerful forces close to the omnipresent epicentre of Chaos."@en ;
    skos:note "That Lovecraft encountered the egregore is a claim about a reception, but the work quotes no wording from it; no pm:Receiving was entered."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Azathoth", "Atazoth", "Astaroth", "Blind Mad God at the Center of Chaos" ;
    pm:nameRole "egregore, one of the elder gods, power source of an octarine conjuration" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAzathothConjuration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "An Azathoth Conjuration: pentacle, blood, the nine-fold name, and glossolalia under a covered gaze"@en, "Eine Azathoth-Beschwörung: Pentakel, Blut, der neunfache Name und Glossolalie mit bedeckten Augen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Octarine magic, its power source Azathoth. The Azathoth pentacle (figure 11) is placed prominently; copious Uranian, solar and Jupiterian incense fills the temple with smoke; the eight-rayed star of Chaos is drawn in the air above and visualised; a statement of intent is made; the pentacle is anointed with fresh blood from the thumb; the operator gazes at or visualises it while meditating on the emergence of sentience from the primeval slime and the quest for the stars, then closes or covers the eyes; vibrates AZATHOTH nine times; shouts AZAK GRIFE DAGARSH AZATHOTH; shouts the letters of the alphabet in random order until harsh glossolalia begins of its own accord; attempts to sink into a deeper susurrating glossolalia; interacts with the egregore; and is banished or exorcised, if necessary by an assistant, until the operators respond to their own names and can perform the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual. In the gnosis the mind works in three modes at once: one part makes glossolalia whose content cannot be remembered, one receives and translates the egregore's communication, one acts as querulant asking things of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: the glossolalia begins with harsh aggressive sounds, taken as the egregore's annoyance at being called, and turns as the gnosis deepens into an incoherent susurrating muttering during which communication takes place mentally, usually recallable at will afterward."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: the banishing is continued until the operators respond to their own names and can perform the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, a criterion an assistant can check."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the egregore will often depart if the eyes are opened, hence blindfold or hood. The departure is an outcome the operator would notice, and it is placed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, pp. 148-149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, The Rite, pp. 149-151; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConversationOfAzathoth a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The knowledge and conversation of Azathoth during possession"@en, "Kenntnis und Gespräch Azathoths während der Besessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The primary application of the conjuration: the knowledge and conversation of Azathoth during possession."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAzathothResultsMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Results magic germane to the nature of Azathoth"@en, "Ergebnismagie gemäß dem Wesen Azathoths"@de ;
    skos:definition "The secondary application of the conjuration: results magic germane to the nature of Azathoth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAzathothConjurationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Azathoth Conjuration is given for the conversation of Azathoth and for results magic germane to it"@en, "Die Azathoth-Beschwörung dient dem Gespräch mit Azathoth und einer ihm gemäßen Ergebnismagie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The conjuration states as its applications the knowledge and conversation of Azathoth during possession and, secondarily, results magic germane to its nature."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAzathothConjuration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicConversationOfAzathoth, pmc:ChaosMagicAzathothResultsMagic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAzathothCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Azathoth is not to be summoned often, for trivial reasons, or against its nature"@en, "Azathoth ist nicht oft, nicht aus nichtigem Grund und nicht gegen sein Wesen zu rufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Azathoth Conjuration should be used infrequently and with caution; the operator should beware of angering the egregore by summoning it for trivial reasons or asking it to do things that conflict with its nature."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicAzathothConjuration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, Rites of a Mixed Nature, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Psychonomicon, ch. 4 Eight Magics, An Azathoth Conjuration, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Appendices: Appendix 1, Liber KKK, introduction and definitions (pp. 155-162).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicLiberKKK a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liber KKK, Kaos Keraunos Kybernetos: twenty-five conjurations, five operations on five levels"@en, "Liber KKK, Kaos Keraunos Kybernetos: fünfundzwanzig Beschwörungen, fünf Operationen auf fünf Ebenen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Liber KKK, Kaos Keraunos Kybernetos, liberally translated as the chaos thunderbolt steers all things, is called the first complete, systematic magical training programme for some centuries and a definitive replacement for the Sacred Magic of Abramelin the Mage, said to be obsolete by its monotheist transcendentalism and its dependence on repressive forms of inhibitory gnosis. It gives general techniques that the magician must develop into a workable programme with whatever symbols, instruments and gnosis appeal; it prescribes no belief except that magic works if certain general principles are followed, and no Chaos magician should slavishly adhere to the fine detail of any system. Any adult may attempt it, and magician applies to either sex. It is a series of twenty-five operations: the five classical conjurations of evocation, divination, enchantment, invocation and illumination, each performed on the five levels of sorcery, shamanic, ritual, astral and high magic, leading from simple practices and the making of tools to complex experiments on the psychic level."@en ;
    skos:note "The judgement on Abramelin names the work but cites no passage of it; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 155; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 156; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicLiberKKKOperation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Liber KKK operation: at least a year in the world, a record of successes only"@en, "Die Operation Liber KKK: mindestens ein Jahr in der Welt, ein Verzeichnis nur der Erfolge"@de ;
    skos:definition "A private temple is highly desirable, but it is essential that the magician remain active in the world for the whole work: there is no retreat, the world is the proving ground, and business and social affairs are the prime focus of the magic, through which the magician defines a personal style or spirituality, spirituality being nothing other than the way one lives. There is no upper limit of time, but the work cannot be completed in less than a year; whoever has time to finish sooner should adopt further worldly commitments as arbitrary goals. The five conjurations of each level may be done in any order, but all five should be completed before the next level. The operation begins on an auspicious or personally significant date. A book records successes with each of the twenty-five conjurations: only successful results are noted, lesser results elsewhere for reference, and the magician modifies the approach to each conjuration until results worthy of recording are achieved. Except perhaps in high magic, every conjuration is planned in detail beforehand, often as a written rubric; the magician may do more than planned but should never fail to carry out what was planned or begin with a vague idea of doing some magic. The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual may be used freely, particularly as prelude and ending to each of the first fifteen conjurations."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: a single recorded success with each of the twenty-five conjurations is the absolute minimum, five successes with each thorough work."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: objective results are called the proof of magic, all else mysticism, and samples of the Philosopher's Stone that do not transmute lead to gold will also fail as elixirs of enlightenment. The absence of objective results is the observable outcome; the work places it on the approach, which is to be modified until results worthy of recording come, and gives the failed result a place in a separate record. The year is a minimum duration of the work, not a deadline after which it counts as failed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 156; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 157; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, pp. 161-162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLifestyleOfRisk a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A lifestyle of risk and uncertainty"@en, "Eine Lebensweise des Wagnisses und der Ungewissheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magic of Liber KKK can only be performed in the context of a lifestyle of risk and uncertainty; the magician may need to adopt projects involving these elements before beginning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 157; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRiskBeforeKKK a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A lifestyle of risk and uncertainty before beginning Liber KKK"@en, "Eine Lebensweise des Wagnisses und der Ungewissheit vor Beginn von Liber KKK"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magic of Liber KKK can only be performed in the context of a lifestyle of risk and uncertainty, and the magician may need to adopt projects involving them before beginning the work."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicLiberKKKOperation ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicLifestyleOfRisk ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Outside a lifestyle of risk and uncertainty the magic of Liber KKK cannot be performed." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 157; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFiveConjurationsDefined a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Evocation, divination, enchantment, invocation and illumination as defined for Liber KKK"@en, "Evokation, Divination, Verzauberung, Invokation und Erleuchtung, wie für Liber KKK bestimmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Evocation is work with entities natural or manufactured, regarded according to taste as independent spirits, fragments of the subconscious or egregores of species; it is usually performed for enchantment, the entities creating effects for the magician, and sometimes for divination. Divination is every practice that extends perception by magical means; enchantment every practice that imposes will on reality. Invocation is the deliberate attunement of consciousness and the unconscious with an archetypal or significant nexus of thought, often a pagan god-form, creating states of inspiration or possession in which enchantment, divination or occasionally evocation are done. Illumination is deliberate self-modification by magic, spells cast at oneself to repair weaknesses or increase strengths, with divination and invocation for inspiration and direction. All operations rest on will, perception and imagination and so are all species of enchantment or divination; imagination is what occurs when will and perception stimulate each other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, pp. 157-158; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, pp. 158-159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryLevel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The sorcery level: simple magic of occult connections between physical phenomena"@en, "Die Ebene der Zauberei: einfache Magie okkulter Verbindungen zwischen physischen Erscheinungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sorcery is simple magic depending on the occult connections between physical phenomena, a mechanical art not requiring the theory of a connection between the operator's mind and the target, any such effect being a bonus. The magician makes artifacts, tools and instruments that interact magically with the physical world and serve again more subtly on the other levels; the work should be thorough, being the foundation of the higher levels. The instruments are best made by the magician's own hand, though existing objects may be adapted if especially significant, unique, designed by the magician or come by in an unusual or meaningful way. Sorcery techniques often resemble childhood behaviour: children have a natural familiarity with the simple principles of magic, and the adult magician seeks to regain that childlike imagination, fluidity and wishful thinking and turn it into something of real power."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Sorcery Level Magic, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The shamanic level: trance, vision, imagination and dream, opening the subconscious"@en, "Die schamanische Ebene: Trance, Vision, Einbildung und Traum, die das Unterbewusste öffnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanic magic works on the level of trance, vision, imagination and dream, opening the subconscious by negating psychic censorship with various techniques."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, pp. 159-160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevelDanger a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Considerable danger on the shamanic level: recourse to sorcery or banishing if it threatens to obsess"@en, "Beträchtliche Gefahr auf der schamanischen Ebene: Rückgriff auf Zauberei oder Bannung, wenn sie zu besetzen droht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician faces considerable danger on the shamanic level and may have frequent recourse to sorcery techniques or banishing rituals if it threatens to obsess or overwhelm."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualLevel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual level: the tools of sorcery and the powers of the shamanic level combined in discipline"@en, "Die rituelle Ebene: die Werkzeuge der Zauberei und die Kräfte der schamanischen Ebene in Zucht verbunden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ritual magic combines the abilities of the sorcery and shamanic levels, bringing the tools of the one together with the subconscious powers liberated on the other in a disciplined and controlled fashion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralLevel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The astral level: magic by visualisation and gnosis alone"@en, "Die astrale Ebene: Magie allein durch Visualisierung und Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Astral magic is performed by visualisation and altered states of consciousness, or gnosis, alone; no physical paraphernalia is used, though the tools of the previous levels can be used as visualised images. At first the magician will probably need seclusion, silence, darkness and considerable effort at concentration and trance to succeed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralMagicAnywhere a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astral magic performed anywhere"@en, "Astrale Magie an jedem Ort"@de ;
    skos:definition "With practice astral magic, which at first needs seclusion, silence and darkness, can be performed anywhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHighMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "High magic: no impediment to will, clairvoyance, prescience or rapport"@en, "Hohe Magie: kein Hindernis für Willen, Hellsehen, Vorauswissen oder Einklang"@de ;
    skos:definition "High magic occurs when there is no impediment to the direct magical effect of will, no barrier to direct clairvoyance and prescience, and no separation between the magician and any rapport or consciousness chosen. For most people its portals open at a few peak moments in a lifetime; as the magician progresses through training, the momentum acquired forces open the gates to the miraculous more often. No procedures are given for its five conjurations: it is where technique gives way to intuitive genius, and each magician must intuit the key. The first twenty conjurations teach the artificial tricks for throwing and catching the magical thunderbolt; in high magic the primordial Chaos at the centre of our being grabs or hurls it by itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, pp. 160-161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKKKTrainingYieldsHighMagic a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Progress through the training is said to open the gates of high magic more often"@en, "Fortschritt in der Ausbildung soll die Tore der hohen Magie öfter öffnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "As the magician progresses through the training, the momentum acquired is said to force open the gates to the miraculous more often than the few peak moments of an ordinary life."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicLiberKKKOperation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicHighMagic ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKKKLevelsInOrder a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "All five conjurations of a level before the next: sorcery, shamanic, ritual, astral, high"@en, "Alle fünf Beschwörungen einer Ebene vor der nächsten: Zauberei, schamanisch, rituell, astral, hoch"@de ;
    skos:definition "The five conjurations on each level may be attempted in any order, but all five should be completed before beginning the next level; the sorcery level is to be done thoroughly, being the foundation on which the higher levels rest, and ritual magic combines the abilities of the sorcery and shamanic levels."@en ;
    skos:note "The node carries the chain of the five levels in order, each dependent level following the one before it; it is prescribed order, since no consequence of skipping a level is stated beyond calling sorcery the foundation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicRitualLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicAstralLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicHighMagic ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicRitualLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicAstralLevel ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGPRAroundConjurations a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual as prelude and ending to the first fifteen conjurations"@en, "Das Gnostische Pentagrammritual als Vorspiel und Abschluss der ersten fünfzehn Beschwörungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "During the operation the magician may need to defend against the results of his or her own mistakes and hostile psychic influences and to replenish health and psychic forces; the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual may be used freely for these purposes and particularly as a prelude and an ending to each of the first fifteen conjurations."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevel, pmc:ChaosMagicRitualLevel ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticPentagramRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber KKK, sorcery level, conjurations one to five (pp. 163-167).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryEvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration one, sorcery evocation: a hand-made fetish treated as a living being"@en, "Erste Beschwörung, Evokation der Zauberei: ein handgemachter Fetisch, wie ein lebendes Wesen behandelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician makes with his or her own hands a physical representation of a fetish entity by carving, moulding or assembly, usually shaped like a living or chimerical being whose form suggests its function, and called a homunculus if vaguely humanoid. It may contain parts of the magician's body or be anointed with blood or sexual fluids. The magician treats it as a living being, speaking his or her will to it, commanding it to exert its influence in the magician's favour and carrying it on critical errands. Some make two fetishes, one to implement will, one to bring knowledge and information."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration One, Sorcery Evocation, pp. 163-164; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFetishPowers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A fetish that attracts success, repels misfortune and holds power"@en, "Ein Fetisch, der Erfolg anzieht, Unglück abwehrt und Kraft speichert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The functions of the fetish entity: in general to attract success, to protect by repelling misfortune and to act as a reservoir of power, or, with a second fetish, to bring knowledge and information."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration One, Sorcery Evocation, p. 164; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryEvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The fetish entity is said to attract success, repel misfortune and store power"@en, "Das Fetischwesen soll Erfolg anziehen, Unglück abwehren und Kraft speichern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The made and commanded fetish is given the functions of attracting success, protecting by repelling misfortune and acting as a reservoir of power, and a second one of bringing knowledge."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryEvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFetishPowers ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration One, Sorcery Evocation, p. 164; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHomunculusNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Homunculus, the fetish entity of vaguely humanoid shape"@en, "Homunculus, das Fetischwesen von entfernt menschlicher Gestalt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A fetish entity made in the first conjuration is known as a homunculus if it is vaguely humanoid in shape."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "homunculus" ;
    pm:nameRole "manufactured fetish entity of vaguely humanoid shape" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration One, Sorcery Evocation, p. 164; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration two, sorcery divination: a simple model of the universe cast for trends and questions"@en, "Zweite Beschwörung, Divination der Zauberei: ein einfaches Modell des Universums für Tendenzen und Fragen geworfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician prepares a simple model of the universe as a divinatory tool: rune sticks or stones are most excellent, occidental geomancy sticks simpler, while tarot or I Ching may prove too complex for the later shamanic work unless abbreviated. Divination is done both for general trends and for specific questions; the elements of the tool are treated as directly related to the parts of reality they represent, and sortilege as a mirror of the process by which reality takes its decisions. It is pitched at a frequency and complexity that allows the answers to be remembered, and preferably for phenomena likely to confirm or negate the divination within a relatively short time."@en ;
    skos:note "The instruction to divine for what will be confirmed or negated soon, and to keep the answers memorable, sets up the comparison of prediction and outcome. The work does not say what is to follow from a negated divination, so it is not counted as a condition of failure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Two, Sorcery Divination, pp. 164-165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryDivinationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The divinatory model is said to answer for trends and specific questions"@en, "Das divinatorische Modell soll über Tendenzen und bestimmte Fragen Auskunft geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sortilege with a simple model of the universe, regarded as a mirror of reality's decisions, is the means of divining general trends and answers to specific questions."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicKnowledgeFromSubconscious ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Two, Sorcery Divination, pp. 164-165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryEnchantment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration three, sorcery enchantment: physical representations of will made with the magical weapon"@en, "Dritte Beschwörung, Verzauberung der Zauberei: physische Darstellungen des Willens, mit der magischen Waffe gefertigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chief among the instruments is a single special tool or weapon of enchantment, a small pointed wand or a knife, also used to trace the pentagrams of the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual; a fist-sized piece of modelling clay may be the only other instrument. The magician makes physical representations of will and desire, using the weapon where possible to make or manipulate them, performing one or several such conjurations a week, and aiming to influence events before nature has made up her mind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Three, Sorcery Enchantment, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicImprobableEventsCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Not too great a strain on nature: no conjuring for highly improbable events"@en, "Der Natur nicht zu viel zumuten: nicht für höchst unwahrscheinliche Ereignisse beschwören"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician should not put too great a strain on nature by conjuring for highly improbable events, and should aim to influence events before nature has made up her mind."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Three, Sorcery Enchantment, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryEnchantmentYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sorcery enchantment is the imposition of will on reality through physical representations"@en, "Verzauberung der Zauberei ist das Auferlegen des Willens auf die Wirklichkeit durch physische Darstellungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Representations of will and desire made and manipulated with the magical weapon are given as the means of imposing will on events not yet decided."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryEnchantment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEffectiveEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Three, Sorcery Enchantment, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration four, sorcery invocation: radical change of behaviour by altering the environment"@en, "Vierte Beschwörung, Invokation der Zauberei: radikale Verhaltensänderung durch veränderte Umgebung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aim is radical change of behaviour by temporarily altering the environment, without limit to the variation of experience arranged: after careful research one might depart in disguise to a strange place and play out a completely new social role, or equip temple and self so as to experience being an ancient Egyptian god for a time. The magician tests to the limit the ability to create arbitrary change by modifying environment and behaviour."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Four, Sorcery Invocation, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicArbitraryChangeByEnvironment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Arbitrary change of behaviour by modifying environment and conduct"@en, "Beliebige Verhaltensänderung durch veränderte Umgebung und veränderte Lebensführung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Radical, arbitrary changes in behaviour created by temporarily altering the environment and one's conduct."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Four, Sorcery Invocation, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "An altered environment and role are said to create radical changes of behaviour"@en, "Eine veränderte Umgebung und Rolle sollen radikale Verhaltensänderungen bewirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Disguise, a new social role or a temple equipped to be a god are given as means of radical behavioural change."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicArbitraryChangeByEnvironment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Four, Sorcery Invocation, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryIllumination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration five, sorcery illumination: the lamp over which oaths and resolutions are proclaimed"@en, "Fünfte Beschwörung, Erleuchtung der Zauberei: die Lampe, über der Eide und Vorsätze verkündet werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician aims for self-improvement in some precisely defined way; grandiose plans for spiritual enlightenment are abandoned in favour of identifying and overcoming obvious weaknesses and increasing existing strengths. An object representing the quest as a whole, technically a lamp, though it may be anything from a ring to a mandala, is made or acquired, and oaths and resolutions are proclaimed over it and may be marked onto its design. Supplementary acts of invocation, enchantment, divination and even evocation may be needed, and it is not unusual to destroy and rebuild the lamp during the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Five, Sorcery Illumination, pp. 166-167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfImprovement a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Precisely defined self-improvement: weaknesses overcome, strengths increased"@en, "Genau bestimmte Selbstverbesserung: Schwächen überwunden, Stärken gemehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Self-improvement in a precisely defined and specific way, by overcoming obvious weaknesses and increasing existing strengths, the aim of the illumination conjurations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Five, Sorcery Illumination, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSorceryIlluminationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Oaths over the lamp are the means of precisely defined self-improvement"@en, "Eide über der Lampe sind das Mittel genau bestimmter Selbstverbesserung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The lamp, the oaths and resolutions proclaimed over it and the supplementary acts are given as the means of self-improvement in illumination."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSorceryIllumination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelfImprovement ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Five, Sorcery Illumination, pp. 166-167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber KKK, shamanic level, conjurations six to ten (pp. 167-172).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicVision a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shamanic magic: connecting mental imagery with phenomena, visions in symbolic language"@en, "Schamanische Magie: Bilder des Geistes mit Erscheinungen verbinden, Visionen in symbolischer Sprache"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanic magic depends on altered states in which active visualisation and passive vision seeking occur most easily; the easiest and safest are half-sleep, dream and light trance from quiet meditation, though any gnosis may be used. It is better to deepen trance by concentrating on visualisation and vision than by extreme gnosis beforehand. The magician seeks to discover and establish connections between mental imagery and phenomena in the world. Visions often speak in symbolic language, diseases appearing as insects or loathsome animals, fears or desires as spirits; the shaman deals with them as the images in which they present themselves, banishing or invoking them by force of visualisation and interpreting their physical meaning. The work tends to become idiosyncratic and free-form, exploring the magician's symbol-synthesising faculties."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Shamanic Level Magic, pp. 167-168; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEcstaticPracticesInitialCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "In initial shamanic exercises, avoid dangerous ecstatic practices that can lead to loss of control"@en, "In den ersten schamanischen Übungen gefährliche ekstatische Praktiken meiden, die zum Kontrollverlust führen können"@de ;
    skos:definition "In initial exercises it is wise to avoid certain dangerous and ecstatic practices which can lead to a loss of control."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicLevel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Shamanic Level Magic, p. 167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicEvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration six, shamanic evocation: a visioned entity projected to do one's bidding"@en, "Sechste Beschwörung, schamanische Evokation: ein geschautes Wesen, ausgesandt, den eigenen Willen zu tun"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician strives to establish a vision of an entity projected to do his or her bidding, often the visualised form of the sorcery fetish. Entities encourage desired events to materialise or seek out information where situations are too complex for simple spells or divinations; they act as semi-intelligent spells with limited independence. The magician builds an increasing rapport with the entities conjured by imagination until they begin to have real effect upon the world; some of the best work with them is done by interacting with them in dreams."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: rapport is built with the imagined entities until they begin to have real effect upon the world."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Six, Shamanic Evocation, p. 168; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSemiIntelligentSpells a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Entities as semi-intelligent spells for situations too complex for simple spells"@en, "Wesen als halbintelligente Zauber für Lagen, die für einfache Zauber zu verwickelt sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Desired events encouraged to materialise and information sought out by entities acting as semi-intelligent spells, where situations are too complex for simple spells or divinations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Six, Shamanic Evocation, p. 168; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicEvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Visioned entities are said to come to have real effect on the world"@en, "Geschaute Wesen sollen wirkliche Wirkung auf die Welt gewinnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rapport built with imagined entities is said to bring them to real effect upon the world, encouraging events and seeking information."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicEvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSemiIntelligentSpells ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Six, Shamanic Evocation, p. 168; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicVisionQuest a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration seven, shamanic divination: the vision quest for an answer sensed in any way"@en, "Siebte Beschwörung, schamanische Divination: die Visionssuche nach einer auf jede Weise empfundenen Antwort"@de ;
    skos:definition "Divination in shamanic magic is a vision quest for answers to particular questions, the answer sensed in any way, hallucinatory voices, tactile sensation or other. The magician concentrates on the question while entering dream, half-sleep or trance and then lets images, voices or sensations arise; the vision may be wholly free-form and interpreted later, or structured by looking for special symbols, particularly those of the sorcery divination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Seven, Shamanic Divination, p. 169; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVisionQuestYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The vision quest is said to give answers to particular questions"@en, "Die Visionssuche soll Antworten auf bestimmte Fragen geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A question held while entering trance is said to be answered by the flow of images, voices and sensations that arises."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicVisionQuest ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicKnowledgeFromSubconscious ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Seven, Shamanic Divination, p. 169; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicEnchantment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration eight, shamanic enchantment: spirit travel to the target and visualised enactment of the desire"@en, "Achte Beschwörung, schamanische Verzauberung: Geistreise zum Ziel und vorgestellte Ausführung des Wunsches"@de ;
    skos:definition "In trance the magician summons an image of the target and visualises the desire coming to pass, often travelling in the spirit vision to the person or situation and enacting in imagination the change of situation or behaviour. Symbolic or distorted elements are generally banished by greater concentration; if persistent they may reveal knowledge of the target or of one's relation to it, and if a person repeatedly appears with an aura or animal form, or a situation has a characteristic vibration or feel, it is often best to work the visualisation directly on that."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Eight, Shamanic Enchantment, pp. 169-170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicEnchantmentYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Visualised enactment in trance is said to impress the will on the world, often succeeding through the target's visioned form"@en, "Vorgestellte Ausführung in Trance soll den Willen der Welt aufprägen, oft erfolgreich über die geschaute Gestalt des Ziels"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanic enchantment is said to impress will upon the world by direct or symbolic visualisation of the desire, and working on a target's visioned aura, animal form or feel is said often to succeed."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicEnchantment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEffectiveEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Eight, Shamanic Enchantment, pp. 169-170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAtavisticInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration nine, shamanic invocation: possession by an animal atavism"@en, "Neunte Beschwörung, schamanische Invokation: Besessenheit durch einen tierischen Atavismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician draws knowledge and power from atavisms, normally animal ones. Explanations offered: the genetic code holds much unused information of evolutionary history; the brain grew by accretion and its older parts share circuits with other animals; some think the psychic part of humans is built from the psychic debris of past beings, others that the collective psyches of animal species are telepathically available. The animal is chosen personally, from childhood affinity, a suggestive characteristic, growing intuition or sudden revelation. The magician visualises himself or herself in animal form in trance, even projects in astral travel as the animal, and acts out its behaviour physically in a suitable environment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Nine, Shamanic Invocation, pp. 170-171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAtavisticKnowledgeAndPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Split consciousness in which the atavism is interrogated and lends its powers"@en, "Gespaltenes Bewusstsein, in dem der Atavismus befragt wird und seine Kräfte leiht"@de ;
    skos:definition "With practice, varying degrees of split consciousness in which the magician can interrogate the atavism on matters it understands and ask it to provide such of its powers as the physical or etheric body can support."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Nine, Shamanic Invocation, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAtavisticInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Practice of atavistic invocation is said to bring split consciousness, knowledge and the atavism's powers"@en, "Übung der atavistischen Invokation soll gespaltenes Bewusstsein, Wissen und die Kräfte des Atavismus bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "With practice the possession by an animal atavism is said to reach split consciousness, in which the atavism answers questions and lends powers within what the bodies can support."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAtavisticInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAtavisticKnowledgeAndPower ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Nine, Shamanic Invocation, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMedicineJourney a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration ten, shamanic illumination: the medicine journey of death, dismemberment and rebirth, or a review in the wilderness"@en, "Zehnte Beschwörung, schamanische Erleuchtung: die Medizinreise aus Tod, Zerstückelung und Wiedergeburt, oder eine Rückschau in der Wildnis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The so-called medicine journey can take many forms. Traditionally it is a death and rebirth experience: the magician visualises his or her own death and the dismemberment of the body, then the rebuilding of body and spirit and a rebirth, sometimes with privations such as sleeplessness, fasting and pain to deepen trance. Another method is a series of visionary journeys summoning the spirits of natural phenomena, animals, plants and stones and asking them to yield knowledge. The simplest is to retire for some days to a wild and secluded place far from human habitation and there conduct a complete review of one's life so far and of one's future expectations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Ten, Shamanic Illumination, p. 172; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSelfKnowledgeAndRenewal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Self-knowledge, self-renewal or self-improvement"@en, "Selbsterkenntnis, Selbsterneuerung oder Selbstverbesserung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aims of the medicine journey, a quest for self-knowledge, self-renewal or self-improvement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Ten, Shamanic Illumination, p. 172; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMedicineJourneyYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The medicine journey is the quest for self-knowledge, self-renewal or self-improvement"@en, "Die Medizinreise ist die Suche nach Selbsterkenntnis, Selbsterneuerung oder Selbstverbesserung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Death and rebirth visualised, journeys to the spirits of nature, or a review of life in the wilderness are given as the forms of the quest for self-knowledge, renewal or improvement."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMedicineJourney ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelfKnowledgeAndRenewal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Ten, Shamanic Illumination, p. 172; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber KKK, ritual magic, conjurations eleven to fifteen (pp. 172-177).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualMagicIndirection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual magic as an indirect approach to desire through sigil and analogy in gnosis"@en, "Rituelle Magie als mittelbare Annäherung an das Begehren durch Sigill und Analogie in der Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "In ritual magic the physical use of instruments is combined with altered states in structured ceremonies, and magical theories are built into the design to make it more precise. Trance is broadened by techniques of gnosis, bringing more fully into play the unconscious parts of the mind, which actually do the magic. Systems of correspondence, analogical thinking and sigils communicate with the unconscious and preoccupy the conscious mind. Ritual magic is always an indirect approach to desire on the conscious level: the magician never works with a direct representation of what he or she wants but with a sigil or symbolic analogy which in gnosis stimulates the actual desire in the unconscious."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Ritual Magic, pp. 172-173; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRitualEvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration eleven, ritual evocation: a material basis charged with a visualised entity and then commanded"@en, "Elfte Beschwörung, rituelle Evokation: eine stoffliche Grundlage, mit einem vorgestellten Wesen geladen und dann befehligt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The entity forms may continue from the earlier levels, come from the classical grimoires, or be built up by the magician. A material basis is always used, even if only a graphic sigil on paper. In the initial evocations a strong visualised image of the entity is built up in full gnosis; in later ones commands and directions are addressed to the material basis, or information is sought from it. The material basis is handled ritually and in gnosis whenever possible and concealed when not in use."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Eleven, Ritual Evocation, pp. 173-174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFourEntitiesLimit a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Not more than four entities maintained at once"@en, "Nicht mehr als vier Wesen zugleich unterhalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Tradition has it that a magician should not seek to maintain more than four entities at the same time, and in practice this seems a good rule of thumb."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicRitualEvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Eleven, Ritual Evocation, p. 173; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualEvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The ritually charged material basis is commanded or questioned as a servitor"@en, "Die rituell geladene stoffliche Grundlage wird als Diener befehligt oder befragt"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the entity is built up in gnosis, commands are addressed to its material basis and information received from it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRitualEvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFourServitors ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Eleven, Ritual Evocation, p. 174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRitualDivination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration twelve, ritual divination: an instrument charged with the question and cast in gnosis"@en, "Zwölfte Beschwörung, rituelle Divination: ein mit der Frage geladenes Instrument, in Gnosis geworfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A physical instrument is manipulated in gnosis to give a symbolic or analogical answer. Deep gnosis tends to preclude complex instruments such as the Kabbala or I Ching for many, very simple ones such as bone casting give too little, and runes, tarot or occidental geomancy are often most useful. Before the divination the instrument is ritually charged with a sigil or analogical representation of the question; the selection is made under gnosis, and the interpretation either under gnosis or on return to ordinary consciousness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Twelve, Ritual Divination, p. 174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualDivinationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The charged instrument cast in gnosis gives a symbolic answer"@en, "Das geladene, in Gnosis geworfene Instrument gibt eine symbolische Antwort"@de ;
    skos:definition "Selection under gnosis from an instrument charged with the question is the means of ritual divination."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRitualDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicKnowledgeFromSubconscious ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Twelve, Ritual Divination, p. 174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRitualEnchantment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration thirteen, ritual enchantment: a spell that occupies the conscious mind, worked in gnosis"@en, "Dreizehnte Beschwörung, rituelle Verzauberung: ein Zauber, der das Bewusstsein beschäftigt, in Gnosis gewirkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The instrument of enchantment from the sorcery level is used to trace sigils in the air and to make and manipulate spells. Every ritual enchantment depends on a spell to occupy and bypass the conscious mind and bring the more powerful unconscious into action, from the making and consecration of a sigil to the manipulation of wax images or the ritual enactment of an analogy of desire. In all cases the magician must use gnosis and concentration upon the spell itself, not the desire it represents, to work effective enchantments."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Thirteen, Ritual Enchantment, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualEnchantmentYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A spell worked in gnosis with concentration on the spell is said to give effective enchantment"@en, "Ein in Gnosis mit Konzentration auf den Zauber gewirkter Zauber soll wirksame Verzauberung geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gnosis and concentration on the spell rather than the desire are named as what works effective enchantments."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRitualEnchantment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEffectiveEnchantment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Thirteen, Ritual Enchantment, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRitualInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration fourteen, ritual invocation: the senses saturated with correspondences of the quality invoked"@en, "Vierzehnte Beschwörung, rituelle Invokation: die Sinne mit den Entsprechungen der invozierten Eigenschaft gesättigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician saturates the senses with experiences corresponding to the quality invoked, dressing temple and person with its colours, smells, symbols, numbers, stones, plants, metals and sounds, and adapting behaviour, thoughts and visualisations in gnosis in order to become possessed by it; the classical god-forms are frequently used, the pagan pantheons summing up the whole of psychology. One should not invoke only qualities one sympathises with; any particularly successful invocation should be followed later by one of quite different qualities, and a thorough programme encompasses success with at least five completely different invocations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Fourteen, Ritual Invocation, pp. 175-176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualInvocationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sense saturation and adapted behaviour in gnosis are said to bring possession by the quality invoked"@en, "Sinnessättigung und angepasstes Verhalten in Gnosis sollen Besessenheit durch die invozierte Eigenschaft bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ritual invocation aims at possession by what is invoked through correspondences and conduct in gnosis."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRitualInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGodFormPossession ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Fourteen, Ritual Invocation, pp. 175-176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRitualIllumination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuration fifteen, ritual illumination: ritual acts applied to oneself, and the choice between Atman and Anatta"@en, "Fünfzehnte Beschwörung, rituelle Erleuchtung: rituelle Akte auf sich selbst gerichtet, und die Wahl zwischen Atman und Anatta"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician applies ritual acts of divination, enchantment, evocation and invocation to himself or herself for specific self-improvement, perhaps with a more elaborate lamp in the form of a mandala of the self or soul. Ritual illumination often forces a choice between Atman and Anatta. Under Anatta, the hypothesis of no-soul, illumination is the addition or deletion of patterns of thought and behaviour. Under Atman, the doctrine of a personal soul or Holy Guardian Angel, the situation is more complex, dangerous and confusing: if a soul without a true will is presumed, the magician can proceed as an Anatta-ist; if a true will is presumed, the conjuration must be directed toward its discovery and implementation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Fifteen, Ritual Illumination, pp. 176-177; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualIlluminationYields a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual acts applied to oneself are the means of specific self-improvement"@en, "Auf sich selbst gerichtete rituelle Akte sind das Mittel bestimmter Selbstverbesserung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ritual divination, enchantment, evocation and invocation applied to oneself are given as the means of specific self-improvement."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRitualIllumination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSelfImprovement ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Fifteen, Ritual Illumination, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTrueWillCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Accept as true will nothing that conflicts radically with common sense, the lower will"@en, "Nichts als wahren Willen annehmen, was dem gesunden Menschenverstand, dem niederen Willen, grundlegend widerspricht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author says he has avoided treading far along the path of the true will but has observed the process go spectacularly wrong in numerous cases; those who attempt it are counselled to avoid accepting as true will anything that conflicts radically with ordinary common sense, called lower will in this paradigm."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the work reports the search for the true will going spectacularly wrong in numerous cases it observed, and names radical conflict with common sense as the mark of a false true will, an outcome the operator could notice; the failure is placed on the operator's acceptance."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicRitualIllumination ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Conjuration Fifteen, Ritual Illumination, p. 177; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber KKK, astral magic, conjurations sixteen to twenty, and high magic,
# conjurations twenty-one to twenty-five (pp. 177-180).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicAstralTempleBuilding a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Building the astral temple and the image of oneself working in it"@en, "Den astralen Tempel und das Bild seiner selbst, der darin arbeitet, aufbauen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A temple or series of temples is erected on the plane of visualised imagination, in any convenient form or as an exact simulacrum of the physical temple, visualised in fine detail with all the equipment for ritual or at least cupboards where instruments can be found; objects visualised into it remain for later inspection unless dissolved or removed. The most important object is the image of oneself working in it. The temple, instruments and moving image of the magician are built up by a repeated series of visualisations until all the details are perfect."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: at first one seems merely to manipulate a puppet of oneself in the temple, but with persistence this should give way to a feeling of actually being there."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, p. 178; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, p. 179; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAstralConjurations a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Astral conjurations: ritual magic performed entirely in a precisely visualised inner landscape"@en, "Astrale Beschwörungen: rituelle Magie, ganz in einer genau vorgestellten inneren Landschaft ausgeführt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Astral magic is ritual magic performed entirely on the plane of visualisation and imagination; unlike the free-form shamanic use of images it requires precise and accurate visualisation of an internal landscape, in which processes are carried out to bring knowledge of the world or to change the world or oneself. It needs at least as much preparation and effort as ritual magic; it is usually begun seated or couched with closed eyes in a quiet place, with few outward signs beyond changes of breathing, posture or expression as gnosis is entered. Each conjuration is planned in advance as in ritual magic, and the five acts take the general form of the ritual ones adapted for astral work."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: without preparation and effort equal to ritual magic it tends to become a brief series of excursions around the imagination to little magical effect. The outcome is named but hardly one the operator could measure; the failure is placed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, pp. 177-178; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, p. 179; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralTempleBeforeAstralMagic a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The astral temple perfected before astral magic proper begins"@en, "Der astrale Tempel vollendet, bevor die eigentliche astrale Magie beginnt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Before beginning astral magic proper the temple, instruments and image of the magician are to be built up until all details are perfect; only when this is complete should the magician begin to use the temple."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicAstralConjurations ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicAstralTempleBuilding ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, p. 179; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExtraordinaryAstralPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astral magic as a source of extraordinary power without equipment"@en, "Astrale Magie als Quelle außerordentlicher Kraft ohne Ausrüstung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Properly performed, astral magic can be a source of extraordinary power, with the advantage of needing no physical equipment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, p. 178; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralConjurationsYield a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Properly performed astral magic is said to be a source of extraordinary power, practicable anywhere with practice"@en, "Richtig ausgeführte astrale Magie soll eine Quelle außerordentlicher Kraft sein, mit Übung überall ausführbar"@de ;
    skos:definition "Astral magic properly prepared is called a source of extraordinary power, and practice is said to free it from the seclusion, silence and darkness it first requires."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAstralConjurations ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicExtraordinaryAstralPower, pmc:ChaosMagicAstralMagicAnywhere ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, Astral Magic, p. 178; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCosmicSpellOfDisbelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The universe's playful spell convincing us we are not magicians"@en, "Der verspielte Zauber des Universums, der uns überzeugt, keine Magier zu sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "All techniques of magic are ways of tricking indefinable parts of ourselves into performing magic; the universe is a magical structure and all are capable of magic. The useful theories are those that explain why magic works so erratically and why we have such inhibitions about believing in it, making it work and recognising that it has worked: it is as if the universe had cast a spell to convince us we are not magicians, a playful cosmic joke that leaves a few cracks for us to shatter the illusion. No details are or can be given for the five conjurations of high magic; magicians rely on the momentum of the four lower levels to carry them into its domain, where they evolve their own tricks and empty-handed techniques for liberating the chaotic creativity within. The appendix closes with the words Kaos Keraunos Kybernetos."@en ;
    skos:note "The theory offered here accounts in advance for magic that does not work and for results not recognised; it is noted with the conditions of failure as a statement about where failure comes from, but it names no outcome and is not counted."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 1, Liber KKK, High Magic, pp. 179-180; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Appendix 2, The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual (pp. 181-185).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticPentagramRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual: the vowels I E A O U vibrated in five body areas, pentagrams at four quarters"@en, "Das Gnostische Pentagrammritual: die Vokale I E A O U in fünf Körperbereichen vibriert, Pentagramme an vier Vierteln"@de ;
    skos:definition "Banishing rituals serve at the beginning and end of longer rituals to establish or re-establish concentration, balance and control, and for visualisation practice or shutting out unwanted influences. The Golden Dawn's lesser banishing ritual of the pentagram is said to be ever less useful, since few are committed enough to Hebraic mysticism to draw power from its god names and angelic images, though its persistence shows the need for such a rite; the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual fulfils all its functions without any sectarian symbol system and serves also for healing. It is named for the ancient schools that designated the magical force of the universe IAO. Procedure: standing facing any direction, the operator inhales fully and exhales slowly sustaining I, a high-pitched ieeeee, while visualising a radiance of energy in the head; then E, lower, in the throat; A, deep, in the heart and lungs, spreading to the muscles of the limbs; O in the belly; U, very deep, in the genital and anal area; then repeats U and works back through O, A, E, I toward the head. Then, exhaling the five sounds in turn, the operator draws with the left arm a strongly visualised pentagram in the air, one bar for each sound, turns a quarter to the left and repeats until back at the start, and finally repeats the opening sequence of radiances. The sounds are vibrated loudly, each for a whole slow exhalation; the IEAOU mantra over the pentagrams serves largely to block discursive thought. The ritual may be elaborated at will, with colours added to the radiances or pentagrams above and below to form a sphere. Its techniques are mantra vibration, visualisation assisted by gesture, and breath control; the body areas correspond to the chakras of some oriental traditions and not others, those systems agreeing little, all being designed to create psychic images of the body for greater psychosomatic control."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: each vowel should produce a physical sensation in the part of the body to which it is attributed, the body played like a musical instrument."@en ;
    skos:note "The judgement on the lesser banishing ritual names the Golden Dawn but cites no text of it; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 2, The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, pp. 181-182; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 2, The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, pp. 182-183; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 2, The Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, Ritual Procedure, pp. 184-185; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

