#
# Appendix 3, Chaos Monasticism (pp. 187-190).
#

pmp:ChaosMagicMonasticObservances a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The observances of a Monk or Nun of Chaos: lesser, greater and extreme, sworn over a staff"@en, "Die Observanzen eines Mönchs oder einer Nonne des Chaos: geringere, größere und äußerste, über einem Stab geschworen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Members of The Pact may at any time elect to follow the observances of the Monks and Nuns of Chaos for as long as it pleases them, never less than a week; an unspecified period is to be ended definitely rather than allowed to lapse. They may be itinerant, the monastery being notionally the whole planet, though a retreat may help the more rigorous forms; the observances are a minimum to which more may be added. The candidate swears an oath over a staff dedicated to magic, no smaller than a walking stick, carried throughout and always close to hand, naming the observances and any personal additions. Lesser: carry the staff at all times, perform a banishing ritual on waking and retiring, keep a full record of dreams, perform a full magical ritual each day, dedicate any sexual gnosis to magic. Greater: the five lesser, a second daily ritual, and the Sigil of Chaos visualised at least once in each waking hour. Extreme: all these, a third daily ritual, and the sigil visualised at least once every hour, night included. The daily rituals may be a Mass of Chaos or a similar act; dedicated sexual gnosis means every sexual act casts spells for divination, invocation or the like; an alarm watch helps the hourly visualisation. The inconvenience of the staff increases vigilance and reminds of the other observances, and it is a badge of office to which other members owe assistance. If the work proceeds satisfactorily the staff is kept as an object of power, engraved with the record of observances, 127 for the lesser for twenty-seven days, 333 for the extreme for thirty-three. The Pact tries to ensure that the Sigil of Chaos is visualised once an hour on the hour somewhere on Earth."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: if the work goes poorly and the observances are largely not met, the staff is to be destroyed. The unmet observances are an outcome the operator can count, and the failure is placed on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 3, Chaos Monasticism, pp. 187-188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 3, Chaos Monasticism, pp. 188-189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 3, Chaos Monasticism, Notes and Observations, pp. 189-190; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

