    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 3, Chaos Monasticism, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 3, Chaos Monasticism, pp. 189-190; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis (pp. 191-216).
#

pmc:ChaosMagicTechniqueOverMetaphysics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Cavalier metaphysics and puritanical technique: the universe confirms most interpretations placed on it"@en, "Unbekümmerte Metaphysik und puritanische Technik: das Universum bestätigt die meisten Deutungen, die man ihm auferlegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most occult traditions have complex, ordered otherworld cosmologies and shambolic techniques. A fundamental insight of Chaos Magic is that sharply delineated technique will work because the universe is more of a shambles than it appears, or, more respectfully, because it has the magical property of confirming most of the interpretations placed upon it, so that even mutually exclusive paradigms can be made to fit. The critical question in the supermarket of belief is therefore how effective the accompanying techniques are: Chaoist magic has a cavalier attitude to metaphysics and a puritanical devotion to empirical technique."@en ;
    skos:note "The traditions are not named; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    skos:note "That the universe confirms most interpretations placed upon it is the work's own reason why a technique cannot discriminate between paradigms; it bears on what could count as a failure and is noted with the counts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, p. 191; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThePact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Magical Pact of the Illuminates of Thanateros: an order teaching technique only"@en, "Der Magische Pakt der Illuminaten von Thanateros: ein Orden, der nur Technik lehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chaoist orthodoxy held cavalier metaphysics and do-it-yourself mythology incompatible with a formal teaching order; this need not be so if only technique is taught, and experience has shown that a formal structure and division of labour encourage productive exchange of expertise. The Pact is an organisational structure for those who wish to do Chaos-type magic together, using a graded hierarchy with checks and balances and glad to admit candidates who rise fast. Chaos Magic is called the major synthesis of the occult renaissance of the last twenty years, and The Pact a prime vehicle to carry it into the next millennium, likely to be more than the Golden Dawn was a century ago. Its formal devices are treated lightly in practice, members styling themselves Frater Vacuity or Soror Impropriety in parody of tradition; the prime functions of the grades are to exclude certain psychotic misanthropes and neurotic creeps and to see that what needs organising is done. The Magical Order of the IOT, functioning as a highly creative disorder, spawned The Pact, a friendly society for mutual support in magic and a vehicle for the Great Work of Magic and its pleasures and profits, and a psychohistoric force in the battle for the aeon."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, pp. 191-193; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Magical Pact of the Illuminates of Thanateros, p. 193; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHierarchicalGambit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The hierarchical gambit, and negative feedback institutionalised in the Insubordinate"@en, "Der hierarchische Gambit, und die negative Rückmeldung, im Insubordinaten eingerichtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "All magical and mystical organisations have used the hierarchical gambit to press for excellence at every level, yet mastery in them often rested on questionable claims to authority from concealed sources rather than technical accomplishment, and the calling of such bluffs undid them. The old mechanism has merit: guru and chela put each other under enormous pressure, and if the chela finally rebels both may gain greatly, though it can as easily end in disaster. Most individuals are relatively sane and competent but most organisations act mad and stupid because they permit only positive feedback from below. The Pact keeps the pressure of hierarchy while overcoming this: all members may volunteer techniques for experiment, the grades merely recognise technical competence and organisational responsibility, higher grades must not comment on the lifestyle, behaviour, tastes or morals of others, and rebellion is institutionalised in the office of Insubordinate, so that a master who attains fair mastery becomes subject to intense criticism, counted a great reward."@en ;
    skos:note "The concealed sources of authority are not named; entered at the claim rather than as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, pp. 193-194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, pp. 194-195; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPactGradesAndOffices a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The grades and offices of The Pact"@en, "Die Grade und Ämter des Pakts"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Pact has four grades, Neophyte 4°, Initiate 3°, Adept 2° and Magus 1°, and five offices: Priest or Priestess of Chaos, a side degree of the 3° or 2°; Supreme Magus, the Head, 0°; Magister Templi, coordinator of a temple, open to any member; Archivist, keeper of a temple's records; and Insubordinate, open to any 3°, a personal assistant who acts as goad, inspector and critic. It is a self-perpetuating oligarchy: advancement is by invitation of those in the grade and above, the Supreme Magus can be replaced only by unanimous action of the 1°, higher grades give organisation, facilities, tuition and material in return for reasonable mundane or magical services, and ultimate appeal lies to the 0°. The Neophyte grade is a period of mutual assessment, either side free to end it; the Initiate ritual marks full acceptance, after which The Pact need not accept a resignation, and the Initiate seeks proficiency in all magic, begins Liber KKK and may work toward the side degree; the Adept ritual marks proficiency and the obligations to teach, defend and administer; the grade of Magus is conferred without a ritual on those of outstanding ability and potential for leadership."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, p. 195; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, p. 196; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Grades and Grade Rituals, pp. 198-199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPactTemples a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Temples of The Pact, sponsored temples, the Magister Templi and the Archivist"@en, "Tempel des Pakts, geförderte Tempel, der Magister Templi und der Archivar"@de ;
    skos:definition "A temple may be founded only by an Adept or Magus, or by an Initiate sponsored by one who periodically inspects it; it is an assembly of members convened wherever privacy is assured, keeps contact addresses that do not reveal membership to outsiders, and takes a characteristic name. A distant group may send representatives to an Adept or Magus with signed letters making the Neophyte assertions, who may be given the 4° and 3° and empowered to open a temple. The Magister Templi, appointed by the sponsor or chosen by the highest grade present, admits only members of the appropriate grade or candidates, receives the signs and words of visitors in private, and may delegate assistants and deputies to lead rituals. The Archivist records time, place, work done and results achieved under formal names or numbers only, encodes but does not encipher confidential matters, and ensures the records are destroyed rather than lost or stolen; Initiates and above of the temple, sponsors and any Magus may inspect them. The minimum furnishing of a temple is the eight-rayed star of Chaos displayed as banner, altar cloth, chaosphere or on a staff; all grades wear plain full robes with sleeves and hoods, commonly black; the silver ring bearing the star may be worn freely but proves neither membership nor grade; members take a one-word name and a number of three or four digits, styled Sor. or Fra. The signs and words of the grades, discreet enough for social use, protect against infiltration and imposture and are periodically changed by the 1°."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Temples of the Pact, pp. 196-197; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, Sponsored Temples, The Office of Magister Templi, The Office of Archivist, pp. 197-198; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Insignia of the Pact, p. 200; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Signs and the Words of the Grades, Notes on the Rituals, pp. 201-202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPactOpeningAndClosing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The opening and closing ritual of a Pact temple"@en, "Das Eröffnungs- und Schlussritual eines Tempels des Pakts"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Magister Templi calls the temple to order and leads the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, then proclaims the temple opened or closed in the grade of Neophyte, Initiate or Adept with the signs and words of that grade, gives them, and proclaims: For the pursuit of the Great Work of Magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Opening Ritual and the Closing Ritual, p. 203; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicNeophyteRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Neophyte ritual: the assertions of the Witches' Pyramid, robe and ring, name and number"@en, "Das Neophytenritual: die Bekenntnisse der Hexenpyramide, Robe und Ring, Name und Zahl"@de ;
    skos:definition "Candidates are accepted after interviews arising from recommendation or petition. The ritual is a marriage to The Pact with divorce permitted at any time; the candidate shows commitment by providing robe and ring to approved specification and is revealed to be of open mind free from dogmatic belief. After asking whether anyone objects and leading the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual, the Magister Templi asks for the assertions of the Witches' Pyramid, to know, to will, to dare, to keep silent: whether the candidate knows there may be no ultimate truths, dares to practise the philosophy and techniques of magic, agrees to keep silent about signs, passwords, business and members' identities, and takes the robe and ring as marks of the will to be a magician; the candidate gives a chosen name and number, is recognised as Neophyte, the temple is opened in that grade, and the ritual ends with raucous cheering and applause."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Grades and Grade Rituals, pp. 198-199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Symbolism of the Grade Rituals, pp. 200-201; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Neophyte Ritual, pp. 204-205; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNeophyteRitualFirst a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The Neophyte ritual before any other ritual or activity of The Pact"@en, "Das Neophytenritual vor jedem anderen Ritual und jeder Tätigkeit des Pakts"@de ;
    skos:definition "No person can be admitted to any other of The Pact's rituals and activities without first having undergone the Neophyte ritual; advancement into each further grade is by invitation, and the Initiate and Adept rituals open with the reaffirmation of the assertions and oaths of the grades below."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicInitiateRitual, pmp:ChaosMagicAdeptRitual ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicNeophyteRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "No person can be admitted to any other of The Pact's rituals and activities without first having undergone the Neophyte ritual." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Grades and Grade Rituals, pp. 198-199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Initiate Ritual, The Adept Ritual, pp. 206-208; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInitiateRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Initiate ritual: submission, the oath of service, and silence"@en, "Das Initiatenritual: Unterwerfung, der Dienst-Eid und Stille"@de ;
    skos:definition "The candidate brings an approved token of Initiate status, a magical weapon, an amulet or a design for the robe. After the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual the candidate reaffirms the four Neophyte assertions in the first person, disrobes and lies flat on the ground while various rituals may be performed, and proclaims: I offer to this Pact such powers of will, imagination, perception and concentration as I possess; I bind myself to the services of this Pact; should I ever break its trust, may I be stripped of all protection. The candidate re-robes, takes the mark, is recognised, and the ritual ends in a few moments of utter silence with all giving the sign of an Initiate, marking total commitment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Symbolism of the Grade Rituals, p. 201; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Initiate Ritual, pp. 206-207; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInitiateBeforeLiberKKK a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The Initiate begins work on Liber KKK"@en, "Der Initiat beginnt die Arbeit an Liber KKK"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Initiate seeks proficiency in all forms of magic and begins work on Liber KKK, and if desired work toward the side degree of Priest or Priestess of Chaos."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as presentation order: within The Pact the work is placed in the Initiate grade, but Liber KKK itself says any adult may attempt it (p. 156)."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicLiberKKKOperation ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicInitiateRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:presentationOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Grades and Grade Rituals, p. 199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAdeptRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Adept ritual: shield, vessel, sword and staff, and unrestrained laughter"@en, "Das Adeptenritual: Schild, Gefäß, Schwert und Stab, und ungezügeltes Lachen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The candidate brings an object to mark adepthood. After the Gnostic Pentagram Ritual and the reaffirmation of the Neophyte assertions and the Initiate oath, the candidate holds the mark aloft and offers himself or herself as a shield for the defence of The Pact and its members, a vessel through which it may pour out teachings of magic, a sword to smite and confound its enemies, and a staff to uphold it, resuming the symbolism of the four elemental weapons, pentacle, cup, sword and wand. The candidate is recognised and the ritual ends in a few moments of mindless unrestrained laughter to lighten the burdens assumed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Symbolism of the Grade Rituals, p. 201; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Adept Ritual, pp. 208-209; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInsubordinate a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Insubordinate: Fool, Jester, Chaplain, Confessor and Inquisitor"@en, "Der Insubordinate: Narr, Spaßmacher, Kaplan, Beichtiger und Inquisitor"@de ;
    skos:definition "Every Magister Templi has a personal Insubordinate chosen by the other members of the temple, and every actively teaching Adept and Magus one chosen by peers. The five duties: as Fool, to see that teachings are comprehensible and to display or pretend ignorance where others pretend to understand; as Jester, to convey criticism with levity and poke fun at what others ignore; as Chaplain, to point out personal failings and blind spots impartially; as Confessor, to receive accounts of personal magical progress without necessarily commenting, a safeguard against sloth or complacency; as Inquisitor, to hold a veto over any instruction and notify the 0° or 1° of its use, to circumvent abuses of position. Insubordinates take a two-word title from these, one for the function most and one for the function least to their temperament, as Inquisitor-Jester. The office lapses on a fresh appointment or when an Initiate holder becomes Adept; some temples rotate it each meeting. Its mark is a necklace displayed when exercising the functions. Business with the Recipient is normally private, and the Recipient may brief the Insubordinate beforehand to avoid a public veto."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, p. 195; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Office of Insubordinate, pp. 210-211; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInsubordinateRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Insubordinate ritual: the necklace, the five questions, and a barrage of insults"@en, "Das Insubordinatenritual: die Halskette, die fünf Fragen und ein Hagel von Beleidigungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "With the temple open in the grade of Initiate or Neophyte, the candidate hands a prepared necklace to the Recipient, who puts it on and, kneeling, asks five times whether the candidate will test him or her as Fool, that all may understand; as Jester, if none else will criticise; as Chaplain, that no fault lie unremedied; as Confessor, lest the Recipient neglect his or her own progress; and as Inquisitor, if the Recipient exceeds his or her authority. The candidate answers I will each time and gives the two-word title; the Recipient gives the necklace to the candidate to remind both of the duties, and the ritual ends with a brief barrage of insulting noises from all at the Recipient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, The Insubordinate Ritual, pp. 212-213; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPactTempleMeeting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The common sequence of a Pact temple meeting"@en, "Die übliche Abfolge einer Tempelversammlung des Pakts"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Magister Templi assures privacy and the grade of visitors, announces apologies and gives briefings. The temple is opened with a grade ritual or the opening ritual. Training and practice follow at the Magister Templi's discretion: mind-control exercises, techniques of gnosis, practice with instruments, lectures, demonstrations and papers. Then magical action: spells and rituals of the five kinds for the needs of The Pact, the temple or individuals, and perhaps the Mass of Chaos as celebration or to ordain a Priest or Priestess of Chaos. Then discussion of administration, planning, personal progress and research, including reports on Liber KKK and communications from other temples. The temple is closed, and refreshment and socialising customarily follow."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, Temple Activities, pp. 214-215; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPactBusiness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Pact business: rituals from memory, approvals for paid work and magical attack, excommunication"@en, "Angelegenheiten des Pakts: Rituale aus dem Gedächtnis, Genehmigungen für bezahlte Arbeit und magischen Angriff, Ausschluss"@de ;
    skos:definition "Few rituals of The Pact use written cues, and any ritual that cannot be committed to memory beforehand is to be regarded as urgently in need of simplification; for complex exercises one member briefs the participants and leads the main sequence. A Magister Templi must obtain prior approval from a Magus before a temple undertakes paid magical work for outsiders or institutions, and before it launches any magical attack, though this may be waived in compelling circumstances. Members of a temple may excommunicate a member for intolerable behaviour by simple majority, the Magister Templi having the casting vote, or the Insubordinate if the Magister Templi is the one excommunicated; excommunicants are barred and not spoken with about magic; wilful treachery may be rewarded with Excommunication with Extreme Prejudice, in which The Pact may adopt a more active response toward the miscreant."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberKaos ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, Pact Business, pp. 215-216; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Kaos (Boston and York Beach: Weiser Books; first published 1992, printing of 2003), Appendix 4, Liber Pactionis, Excommunication, p. 216; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

